# Sidecar L4DEL annotations for openai/math (Lean sources under lean/). Paths are relative to the lean root.
# The ComparatorChallenges files state a result with a `sorry` body on purpose: the matching .json names
# the solution module (under OAI/) that the Comparator tool checks against the same statement.

@file ComparatorChallenges/QuasiRiemannHypothesis.lean
@decl OAI.riemannZeta_ne_zero_of_seven_eighths_lt_re
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Zero-free half-plane for the Riemann zeta function
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Riemann zeta function has no zeros anywhere to the right of the vertical line with real part 7/8.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement takes a complex s with real part strictly greater than 7/8 and concludes riemannZeta s is not zero.
-- L4DEL: step: In this challenge file the proof body is sorry; the paired JSON file names the solution module that the Comparator tool checks against this statement.
-- L4DEL: step: The manuscript proves a broader family of zero-free statements; this declaration isolates only the Riemann-zeta consequence and does not formalize the full argument in the challenge file.
-- L4DEL: term: Comparator challenge = a file that states a theorem with a sorry body so the Comparator tool can check that a separate solution module proves exactly that statement
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI quasi-Riemann manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-September-30-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 003 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/003.md
-- L4DEL: source: Bernhard Riemann (1859), David Wilkins translation, p. 4 = https://www.claymath.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/Wilkins-translation.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI quasi-Riemann manuscript (abstract) = the Riemann zeta function is zero-free in this half-plane
-- L4DEL: quote: Bernhard Riemann (1859), David Wilkins translation, p. 4 = it is very probable that all roots are real. Certainly one would wish for a stricter proof here

@file ComparatorChallenges/DirichletSevenEighths.lean
@decl OAI.DirichletCharacter.LFunction_ne_zero_of_seven_eighths_lt_re
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Zero-free half-plane for Dirichlet L-functions
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every Dirichlet L-function, for every modulus and character, is nonzero to the right of real part 7/8, apart from the pole of the trivial character at 1.
-- L4DEL: step: The hypotheses are a modulus q, a Dirichlet character chi, a point s with real part above 7/8, and the exclusion of the case chi = 1 and s = 1.
-- L4DEL: step: The conclusion is chi.LFunction s is not zero; this challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: step: The exception removes the principal-character pole rather than permitting a zero; the theorem is uniform in q and chi but says nothing about the remaining strip at or left of 7/8.
-- L4DEL: term: Dirichlet L-function = the analytic continuation of the Dirichlet series sum chi(n) n^(-s) attached to a Dirichlet character chi
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI quasi-Riemann manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Quasi-Riemann-Hypothesis-September-30-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 003 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/003.md
-- L4DEL: source: G. Lejeune Dirichlet (1837), original paper scan = https://www.e-rara.ch/download/pdf/5688045.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI quasi-Riemann manuscript (abstract) = all Dirichlet L-functions are zero-free in the half-plane ℜs > 7/8

@file ComparatorChallenges/SiegelZeros.lean
@decl OAI.SiegelZeros.WeightedTorusJets.exists_absolute_real_zero_gap
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Uniform gap for real zeros of L-functions
-- L4DEL: intuition: There is one positive constant c such that any real zero beta of a primitive real Dirichlet L-function of conductor q satisfies 1 - beta at least c / log q.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement obtains a single c greater than 0, with no explicit value given.
-- L4DEL: step: For every q at least 3, every primitive nonprincipal character chi taking real values, and every real zero beta strictly between 0 and 1, it concludes c <= (1 - beta) * log q.
-- L4DEL: step: This bounds real zeros away from 1, in the shape of a Landau-Siegel zero exclusion; the challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: term: Landau-Siegel zero = a hypothetical real zero of a Dirichlet L-function very close to 1
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Landau-Siegel manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Uniform-exclusion-of-Landau-Siegel-zeros-October-1-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 003 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/003.md
-- L4DEL: source: Carl Ludwig Siegel (1935), original paper = https://doi.org/10.4064/aa-1-1-83-86
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Landau-Siegel manuscript (abstract) = every real zero β ∈ (0, 1) of every primitive nonprincipal real Dirichlet L-function of conductor q ≥ 3

@file ComparatorChallenges/HadwigerCounterexample.lean
@decl OAI.HadwigerCounterexample.not_hadwiger_conjecture
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Hadwiger's conjecture fails
-- L4DEL: intuition: The statement denies that every finite graph's chromatic number is at most its largest clique-minor size.
-- L4DEL: step: Graphs are simple graphs on Fin m, and hadwigerNumber is the supremum of t such that the graph has a clique minor of order t (connected disjoint branch sets, pairwise touching).
-- L4DEL: step: The statement is the negation of "for all m and all graphs G on Fin m, chromaticNumber G is at most hadwigerNumber G".
-- L4DEL: step: This challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: term: clique minor = a family of disjoint connected vertex sets in a graph, every two of which have an edge between them
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Hadwiger-counterexample manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 157 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/157.md
-- L4DEL: source: Hugo Hadwiger (1943), original-paper bibliographic record = https://cir.nii.ac.jp/crid/1570009751125987840
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Hadwiger-counterexample manuscript (abstract) = We disprove Hadwiger’s conjecture

@decl OAI.HadwigerCounterexample.exists_counterexamples
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Counterexamples of arbitrarily large order
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every bound M there is a graph on m >= max(M, 5) vertices with independence number at most 2 whose chromatic number is above its Hadwiger number.
-- L4DEL: step: The graph G on Fin m has independence number at most 2.
-- L4DEL: step: Its connected matching number is below m/100 and its Hadwiger number is below 26m/75 + 2/3, which is itself below m/2.
-- L4DEL: step: The chromatic number is finite, is at least m/2, and exceeds the Hadwiger number; the challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Hadwiger-counterexample manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-counterexample-to-Hadwigers-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 157 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/157.md
-- L4DEL: source: Hugo Hadwiger (1943), original-paper bibliographic record = https://cir.nii.ac.jp/crid/1570009751125987840
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Hadwiger-counterexample manuscript (abstract) = constructing arbitrarily large graphs whose chromatic number exceeds their Hadwiger number

@file ComparatorChallenges/KaplanskyDirectFiniteness.lean
@decl OAI.KaplanskyCounterexample.MainClaim
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Failure of direct finiteness in characteristic 2
-- L4DEL: intuition: A group algebra over a finite field of characteristic 2, of a finitely generated group, can contain elements a, b with a*b = 1 but b*a different from 1.
-- L4DEL: step: The claim has the form: there are a finite field K with CharP K 2, a finitely generated group G, and a, b in MonoidAlgebra K G.
-- L4DEL: step: Those satisfy a * b = 1 and b * a != 1.
-- L4DEL: step: This Lean abstraction asks only for finite generation; the manuscript's stronger finitely presented, odd-torsion construction supplies such a witness but is not repeated in MainClaim's type.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Kaplansky counterexample manuscript (p. 1) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 197 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/197.md
-- L4DEL: source: Jan Trlifaj (1990), p. 427 = https://dml.cz/bitstream/handle/10338.dmlcz/106877/CommentatMathUnivCarol_031-1990-3_4.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Kaplansky counterexample manuscript (p. 1) = a finite field K of characteristic two, a finitely presented group G
-- L4DEL: quote: Jan Trlifaj (1990), p. 427 = A well-known conjecture of Kaplansky states that for any group G and any field K the group ring KG is directly finite

@decl OAI.KaplanskyCounterexample.main_theorem
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Main theorem of the Kaplansky counterexample
-- L4DEL: intuition: States that MainClaim holds; the challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: step: The theorem is just MainClaim, a statement about one-sided inverses in a group algebra.
-- L4DEL: step: Expanding MainClaim reveals existential witnesses K, G, a, and b; main_theorem introduces no new hypotheses or weaker replacement conclusion.
-- L4DEL: step: The pinned manuscript gives the stronger concrete construction and a terminating prescription, while this comparator target checks the compact existential proposition.
-- L4DEL: prereq: OAI.KaplanskyCounterexample.MainClaim@ComparatorChallenges/KaplanskyDirectFiniteness.lean
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Kaplansky counterexample manuscript (p. 1) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-Counterexample-to-Kaplanskys-Direct-Finiteness-Conjecture-in-Characteristic-Two-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 197 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/197.md
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Kaplansky counterexample manuscript (p. 1) = The field, group, and finite sums are specified by a terminating prescription

@file ComparatorChallenges/MahlerConjecture.lean
@decl OAI.SymmetricMahler.symmetric_mahler
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Symmetric Mahler volume bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: For a centrally symmetric convex body K in n dimensions, volume(K) times volume(its polar) is at least 4^n / n!.
-- L4DEL: step: K is compact, convex, symmetric under x to -x, with nonempty interior, in Fin n to R with n at least 1.
-- L4DEL: step: coordinatePolar K is the set of p with the sum of p_i v_i at most 1 for all v in K.
-- L4DEL: step: The conclusion compares the product of the two Lebesgue volumes (taken as real numbers) with 4^n / n!; the challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: term: polar body = the set of vectors p whose pairing with every point of K is at most 1
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI symmetric-Mahler manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-symmetric-Mahler-conjecture-and-its-equality-cases-September-22-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 087 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/087.md
-- L4DEL: source: Kurt Mahler (1939), p. 96 = https://dml.cz/dmlcz/109441
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI symmetric-Mahler manuscript (abstract) = Every origin-symmetric convex body in Rn has volume product at least 4n /n!
-- L4DEL: quote: Kurt Mahler (1939), p. 96 = Wahrscheinlich lauten die exacten Ungleichungen

@file ComparatorChallenges/AffineBernstein.lean
@decl OAI.AffineBernstein.affine_bernstein
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Affine Bernstein theorem in dimensions 3 to 9
-- L4DEL: intuition: A complete, smooth, uniformly convex solution of the affine maximal surface equation on a convex domain must be a paraboloid.
-- L4DEL: step: The hypotheses fix n between 3 and 9, an open convex nonempty domain, smooth u with positive definite Hessian, the affine maximal equation holding, and completeness for the graph's Euclidean path metric.
-- L4DEL: step: The conclusion has three parts: the domain is the whole space, u is a quadratic polynomial with symmetric positive definite matrix, and an affine equivalence carries the standard paraboloid onto the graph of u.
-- L4DEL: step: The challenge file leaves the proof as sorry.
-- L4DEL: prereq: OAI.AffineBernstein.AffineMaximalOn@ComparatorChallenges/AffineBernstein.lean, OAI.AffineBernstein.EuclideanGraphComplete@ComparatorChallenges/AffineBernstein.lean
-- L4DEL: term: affine maximal = satisfying the fourth-order equation that the cofactor-Hessian contracted with the Hessian of det(D2u)^(-(n+1)/(n+2)) vanishes
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI affine-Bernstein manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-affine-Bernstein-theorem-in-dimensions-three-through-nine-September-24-2026/main.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 353 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/353.md
-- L4DEL: source: Neil S. Trudinger and Xu-Jia Wang (2000), abstract = https://hdl.handle.net/1885/90182
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI affine-Bernstein manuscript (abstract) = a smooth locally uniformly convex affine maximal graph is an elliptic paraboloid whenever its induced Euclidean metric is complete
-- L4DEL: quote: Neil S. Trudinger and Xu-Jia Wang (2000), abstract = an affine maximal graph of a smooth, locally uniformly convex function on two dimensional Euclidean space, R2, must be a paraboloid

@file ComparatorChallenges/MatrixMultiplication.lean
@decl OAI.MatrixMultiplication.complex_omega_le_nine_quarters
-- L4DEL: title: Complex matrix multiplication has exponent at most 9/4
-- L4DEL: intuition: This is an asymptotic arithmetic-complexity statement: for every positive slack `ε`, sufficiently large complex `n×n` matrices can be multiplied using `O(n^(9/4+ε))` additions, subtractions, and multiplications in a division-free program. It does not claim that the construction is fastest at practical matrix sizes or in bit complexity.
-- L4DEL: step: The manuscript first bounds the asymptotic tensor rank of matrix multiplication by analysing restrictions and asymptotic rates.
-- L4DEL: step: Those tensor identities yield algorithms at every exponent strictly above `9/4`; the formal definition of `ω` then turns the family of bounds into `ω(ℂ) ≤ 9/4`.
-- L4DEL: step: The Lean target is the exact square-exponent conclusion. Its comparator configuration points to the released solution module, while the pinned manuscript supplies the mathematical exposition.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI 9/4 manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Matrix-Multiplication-Nine-Fourths-October-2-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 107 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/107.md
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI mathematics release announcement = https://openai.com/index/sharing-ai-progress-in-mathematics/
-- L4DEL: source: Avi Wigderson and Binyi Zu (2023), page 3 = https://www.math.ias.edu/~avi/PUBLICATIONS/WigdersonZu_Final_Draft_Oct2023.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI 9/4 manuscript (abstract) = We prove that the exponent of matrix multiplication over the complex numbers is at most 9/4.
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI mathematics release announcement = We’re releasing a broad range of new mathematical results produced by an internal frontier model.
-- L4DEL: quote: Avi Wigderson and Binyi Zu (2023), page 3 = natural, obvious and centuries-old methods for solving natural, important computational problems may be far from the fastest.

@file ComparatorChallenges/MatrixMultiplication.lean
@decl OAI.MatrixMultiplication.complex_alpha_gt_93_div_200
-- L4DEL: title: The complex dual matrix-multiplication exponent is greater than 0.465
-- L4DEL: intuition: The dual exponent `α` is the supremum of aspect ratios `k` for which multiplying an `n×n^k` matrix by an `n^k×n` matrix can be done with exponent 2, up to arbitrary positive slack. This theorem proves `α > 93/200 = 0.465`; it is a strict asymptotic bound, not a benchmark at a fixed dimension.
-- L4DEL: step: The accompanying manuscript develops rectangular matrix-multiplication bounds over characteristic-zero fields.
-- L4DEL: step: A concrete construction attains exponent 2 for an aspect ratio strictly larger than `0.465`.
-- L4DEL: step: The Lean target states that strict lower bound directly, with the comparator selecting the released matrix-multiplication solution module.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI rectangular matrix-multiplication manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 107 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/107.md
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI mathematics release announcement = https://openai.com/index/sharing-ai-progress-in-mathematics/
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI rectangular matrix-multiplication manuscript (abstract) = the dual exponent satisfies α > 0.465

@file ComparatorChallenges/MatrixMultiplication.lean
@decl OAI.MatrixMultiplication.complex_rectangular_omega_lt_523_div_250
-- L4DEL: title: Rectangular complex matrix multiplication at aspect ratio 0.709 has exponent below 2.092
-- L4DEL: intuition: For multiplication of an `n×n^0.709` matrix by an `n^0.709×n` matrix over `ℂ`, the arithmetic exponent is strictly less than `523/250 = 2.092`. The strict inequality means some uniform positive exponent improvement exists; it does not specify a practical crossover size.
-- L4DEL: step: The manuscript turns tensor constructions into explicit upper bounds for rectangular matrix multiplication in characteristic zero.
-- L4DEL: step: Substituting aspect ratio `709/1000` yields a bound strictly below `523/250`.
-- L4DEL: step: The Lean target isolates that numerical consequence, while the pinned formalization note records its intended arithmetic model and scope.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI rectangular matrix-multiplication manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026/Complex-Matrix-Multiplication-Below-2.258-and-Rectangular-Bounds-September-24-2026.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 107 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/107.md
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI mathematics release announcement = https://openai.com/index/sharing-ai-progress-in-mathematics/
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI rectangular matrix-multiplication manuscript (abstract) = ω(1, 0.709, 1) < 2.092

@file ComparatorChallenges/Catalan.lean
@decl OAI.InternalCatalan.catalan_irrational
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Catalan's constant is irrational
-- L4DEL: intuition: Catalan's constant is the alternating reciprocal-square sum G = Σ(j ≥ 0) (-1)^j/(2j+1)^2, equivalently the Dirichlet beta value β(2). This declaration says that no rational number equals that real series.
-- L4DEL: step: Lean's `Irrational` predicate means there is no rational r whose coercion to the reals equals the infinite sum.
-- L4DEL: step: The displayed summand alternates sign with j and divides by the square of the odd integer 2j+1, so the target is the standard Catalan constant rather than the unrelated Catalan numbers.
-- L4DEL: step: The challenge file itself ends in `sorry`; its comparator configuration selects `OAI.NumberTheory.Catalan.Main` as the separate released solution module checked against this exact statement.
-- L4DEL: term: Catalan's constant = the special value β(2) = Σ(j ≥ 0) (-1)^j/(2j+1)^2 of the Dirichlet beta function
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Catalan manuscript (Theorem 1.1) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Catalans-constant-is-irrational-September-24-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 005 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/005.md
-- L4DEL: source: Tanguy Rivoal and Wadim Zudilin (2003), p. 1 = https://rivoal.perso.math.cnrs.fr/articles/betacatalan.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Catalan manuscript (Theorem 1.1) = Catalan’s constant is irrational.
-- L4DEL: quote: Tanguy Rivoal and Wadim Zudilin (2003), p. 1 = the equivalent of Apéry's theorem “ζ(3) ∉ Q” for Catalan's constant has not yet been proved

@file ComparatorChallenges/PiExponent.lean
@decl OAI.PiExponent.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: The irrationality exponent of π is exactly 2
-- L4DEL: intuition: Rational numbers approximate π infinitely often at essentially the Dirichlet rate q^(-2), but no exponent strictly larger than 2 succeeds infinitely often. The two conjuncts state both the eventual lower bound and the exact supremum characterization.
-- L4DEL: step: For every real ν > 2, there is an integer threshold Q ≥ 2 such that every integer denominator q ≥ Q and every integer numerator p satisfy q^(-ν) ≤ |π - p/q|.
-- L4DEL: step: Independently, Lean forms the set of positive exponents ν for which infinitely many rationals r of denominator at least 2 have 0 < |π-r| < denominator(r)^(-ν), and states that its supremum is 2.
-- L4DEL: step: The challenge file proves neither conjunct locally because its body is `sorry`; the comparator checks the exact conjunction against `OAI.NumberTheory.PiExponent.Main`. The manuscript's Flint–Hills corollary is not part of this target.
-- L4DEL: term: irrationality exponent = the supremum of exponents ν for which an irrational number has infinitely many rational approximations with error below q^(-ν)
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI π irrationality-exponent manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-irrationality-exponent-of-pi-is-2-September-24-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 017 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/017.md
-- L4DEL: source: Michel Waldschmidt (2004), p. 265 = https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~michel.waldschmidt/articles/pdf/odp.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Kurt Mahler (1953), first explicit finite irrationality measure for π = https://ems.press/books/dms/252/4986
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI π irrationality-exponent manuscript (abstract) = the irrationality exponent of π is 2

@file ComparatorChallenges/DyadicAvoidance.lean
@decl OAI.Problem310.dyadic_affine_avoidance
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: A large compact set avoiding every affine dyadic sequence
-- L4DEL: intuition: No matter how small a measure loss η is allowed, one can keep a compact subset of [0,1] with measure greater than 1-η while destroying every translated and nonzero-rescaled copy of 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, and so on.
-- L4DEL: step: Given 0 < η < 1, the theorem produces a compact E contained in the closed unit interval whose Lebesgue volume is greater than `ENNReal.ofReal (1-η)`.
-- L4DEL: step: For every translation x and every real scale s ≠ 0, there is an index n ≥ 1 for which x + s·2^(-n) is outside E; negative as well as positive scales are covered.
-- L4DEL: step: Thus E contains no complete affine copy of the infinite dyadic sequence. The challenge body is `sorry`, while the comparator checks this exact target against `OAI.MeasureTheory.DyadicAvoidance.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: affine copy = a translated and nonzero-rescaled set x+sA
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI dyadic Erdős-similarity manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-dyadic-case-of-the-Erdos-similarity-conjecture-September-25-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 084 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/084.md
-- L4DEL: source: Paul Erdős (1974), original problem bibliographic record = https://www.ime.usp.br/~yoshi/ERDOS/Erdos_fixed.html
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI dyadic Erdős-similarity manuscript (abstract) = contains no affine copy of the dyadic sequence {2−n : n ≥ 1}

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Target.PermutationTable
-- L4DEL: title: A finite permutation table with a certified inverse
-- L4DEL: intuition: A `PermutationTable alphabet` stores both directions of a permutation of `Fin alphabet`: `images` is the forward table and `inverseImages` is its proposed inverse.
-- L4DEL: step: Both vectors have exactly `alphabet` entries, so every label has a forward image and an inverse image without a partial lookup.
-- L4DEL: step: `leftInverse` and `rightInverse` prove the two tables undo each other in both orders. Those fields are what make this data a permutation rather than merely two arrays.
-- L4DEL: step: The later output encoding serializes only `images`; the inverse table and proofs certify the Lean object but do not add duplicate words to `gameBits`. The manuscript supports explicit permutation tables, while this two-table certification is the Lean implementation.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L7-L13
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Subhash Khot, On the Unique Games Conjecture (2010), p. 5 = https://doi.org/10.1109/CCC.2010.19
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = including the table of each permutation.
-- L4DEL: quote: Subhash Khot, On the Unique Games Conjecture (2010), p. 5 = fixing a label to v uniquely fixes the label to w and vice versa

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Target.Instance
-- L4DEL: title: An explicit, unweighted list of Unique Games constraints
-- L4DEL: intuition: An `Instance alphabet` records a finite number of vertices and a nonempty list of permutation constraints over that fixed alphabet.
-- L4DEL: step: Each constraint carries endpoints in `Fin vertices`, so malformed vertex indices cannot inhabit the structure.
-- L4DEL: step: Constraints are list occurrences rather than a set: identical or parallel entries remain separate and are counted with multiplicity by `countSatisfied`.
-- L4DEL: step: There is no weight field. Thus the ratio used later is the ordinary number of satisfied list entries divided by the nonzero list length.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L24-L35
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = a finite vertex set, a finite alphabet K, and a nonempty list of oriented constraints

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryFormula.Formula
-- L4DEL: title: Binary 3SAT as a finite list of three-literal clauses
-- L4DEL: intuition: A `Formula` is exactly a list of `Clause`s, and each clause is a length-three vector of signed literals whose variable names are natural numbers.
-- L4DEL: step: Variable names may be sparse, and neither repeated names nor repeated literals are forbidden. This is a syntax container, not a promise that the input has been simplified.
-- L4DEL: step: Satisfaction is defined separately: one Boolean assignment must make every listed clause evaluate to `true`.
-- L4DEL: step: The manuscript starts its final construction from a 3SAT formula. The particular framed bit serialization below is a Lean comparator interface and is not attributed to the paper.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L43-L62
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 38 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 38 = Given a 3SAT formula, apply Theorem 4.1 and Lemma 4.2

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryEncoding.parseName
-- L4DEL: title: Decode one canonically framed natural-number name
-- L4DEL: intuition: `parseName` reads one framed list of bits, converts it to a natural number, and returns that number together with the unconsumed suffix only when the digits are canonical.
-- L4DEL: step: `parseFrame` uses `false` as the end marker and `true` followed by a bit as one framed digit.
-- L4DEL: step: `bitsValue` reconstructs the natural number; comparing the parsed digits with `name.bits` rejects padded or otherwise alternate encodings of the same number.
-- L4DEL: step: This parser is a comparator-level serialization choice. The manuscript asserts a deterministic polynomial-time reduction but does not specify this framing, so no manuscript quotation is attached to the encoding detail.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L70-L85
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 102 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/102.md

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryEncoding.parseClauses
-- L4DEL: title: Decode a terminated clause list with structural fuel
-- L4DEL: intuition: `parseClauses fuel input` parses a list in which `false` ends the list and `true` announces one three-literal clause followed by the rest.
-- L4DEL: step: Zero fuel always fails; positive fuel plus `false` succeeds with the empty clause list; positive fuel plus `true` parses one clause and recurses with one less unit.
-- L4DEL: step: An empty input without a terminator fails. `decodeFormula` supplies `input.length + 1` fuel, enough to bound clause iterations while each framed variable name may consume several bits.
-- L4DEL: step: This is termination and canonical-input machinery for the Lean interface, not a proof step quoted from the mathematical manuscript.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L93-L111
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 102 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/102.md

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Complexity.MachineFiniteAlphabet.FiniteAlphabet
-- L4DEL: title: Every physical tape of the reduction machine has a finite alphabet
-- L4DEL: intuition: `FiniteAlphabet M` means `Finite (M.Γ k)` for every physical tape index `k` of the two-tape-style finite Turing-machine model.
-- L4DEL: step: The quantifier ranges over tape indices, not game labels. Together with the machine's finite number of tapes, it rules out hiding an infinite symbol type inside the polynomial-time computation.
-- L4DEL: step: This condition does not give one numeric bound on every alphabet and does not itself prove polynomial time; `BinaryGapReduction.computation` carries the separate runtime contract.
-- L4DEL: step: The paper's fixed finite game alphabet and this machine-alphabet condition are related explicitness requirements but are not the same proposition.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L172-L178
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 102 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/102.md

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Explicit.MachineOutputContract.SimpleBipartite
-- L4DEL: title: A certificate that the output graph is simple and bipartite
-- L4DEL: intuition: `SimpleBipartite game` assigns every vertex a Boolean side, orients every constraint from the `false` side to the `true` side, and forbids two occurrence indices from sharing the same ordered endpoints.
-- L4DEL: step: `sourceSide` and `targetSide` certify the bipartition for every list occurrence rather than merely asserting that some partition exists.
-- L4DEL: step: `endpoints_injective` is injectivity on occurrence indices. Because all constraints use the same left-to-right orientation, it gives at most one constraint for each opposite-side vertex pair.
-- L4DEL: step: The certificate permits isolated vertices; simplicity and bipartiteness do not require connectivity or positive degree.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L188-L198
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = A simple bipartite instance has two disjoint vertex sets and at most one constraint on each pair of opposite vertices.

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Explicit.MachineOutputContract.BinaryGapReduction
-- L4DEL: title: The complete machine-checkable contract for the gap reduction
-- L4DEL: intuition: A `BinaryGapReduction ε δ` packages one alphabet, binary-vector-space coordinate system, constructor, machine computation, and the two gap guarantees before any encoded formula is supplied.
-- L4DEL: step: `alphabetAtLeastTwo`, `dimensionPositive`, and `coordinates` identify the fixed labels with `Fin dimension → ZMod 2`; `translations` then requires every output constraint to add one fixed binary shift.
-- L4DEL: step: `construct` returns a nonempty unweighted instance, while `simpleBipartite` certifies its graph shape. `computation` and `finiteAlphabet` expose the exact output-bit function as a finite-alphabet polynomial-time Turing computation.
-- L4DEL: step: Completeness says a satisfiable decoded input has some labeling satisfying at least `1-ε` of the occurrences. Soundness says an unsatisfiable decoded input makes every labeling satisfy at most `δ` of them.
-- L4DEL: step: The record contains the theorem's output contract; it is not itself the existence proof. `theorem11` is the declaration that states such a record is nonempty for every allowed pair of errors.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L200-L222
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = The alphabet, the degree of the running-time polynomial, and its constants depend only on ε, δ.

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Target.Constraint
-- L4DEL: title: One oriented permutation constraint
-- L4DEL: intuition: A `Constraint vertices alphabet` selects a source vertex, a target vertex, and the permutation table that tells which target label matches each source label.
-- L4DEL: step: Both endpoints inhabit `Fin vertices`, so they are valid indices into the same finite vertex set.
-- L4DEL: step: Orientation matters: satisfaction applies the stored permutation to the source label and compares the result with the target label.
-- L4DEL: step: This structure is one occurrence's mathematical data. Multiplicity and nonemptiness belong to `Instance`, not to an individual constraint.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L15-L18
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = where πe is a permutation of K.

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Target.Constraint.satisfied
-- L4DEL: title: Test whether one labeling satisfies one constraint
-- L4DEL: intuition: `constraint.satisfied labeling` is the Boolean decision that the target vertex's label equals the forward image of the source vertex's label.
-- L4DEL: step: Read `labeling constraint.source`, apply `constraint.permutation.images`, and compare with `labeling constraint.target`.
-- L4DEL: step: `decide` converts that decidable equality into `true` or `false`; it does not search for a labeling.
-- L4DEL: step: Because the table has a certified inverse, fixing either endpoint label uniquely determines the satisfying label at the other endpoint.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L20-L22
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = A labeling a satisfies e when a(ve ) = πe (a(ue )).

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Target.countSatisfied
-- L4DEL: title: Count satisfied constraint occurrences
-- L4DEL: intuition: `countSatisfied labeling constraints` recursively counts how many entries in the constraint list evaluate to `true` under one labeling.
-- L4DEL: step: The empty list contributes zero.
-- L4DEL: step: For a head constraint, add one when `constraint.satisfied labeling` is true and zero otherwise, then recurse on the tail.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an occurrence count: repeated equal constraints are counted repeatedly. The later game value divides this natural number by the nonempty list length.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L31-L35
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = Its value is the fraction of satisfied constraints

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryFormula.Literal
-- L4DEL: title: A signed Boolean variable occurrence
-- L4DEL: intuition: A `Literal` pairs an arbitrary natural-number variable name with a Boolean sign.
-- L4DEL: step: `positive = true` represents the variable itself; `positive = false` represents its Boolean negation.
-- L4DEL: step: Names are natural numbers rather than a dense finite type, so an encoded formula may use sparse indices.
-- L4DEL: step: `DecidableEq` lets the surrounding syntax compare literals mechanically; it adds no well-formedness restriction.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L43-L46

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryFormula.Literal.eval
-- L4DEL: title: Evaluate a signed literal under a Boolean assignment
-- L4DEL: intuition: Given `assignment : Nat → Bool`, a positive literal returns the assigned bit and a negative literal returns its Boolean complement.
-- L4DEL: step: Look up the variable by `literal.name`.
-- L4DEL: step: If `literal.positive` is true, keep the value; otherwise apply Boolean `!`.
-- L4DEL: step: The assignment is total on all natural names, so sparse or previously unseen indices still have a defined value.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L48-L49

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryFormula.Clause
-- L4DEL: title: Exactly three literal slots
-- L4DEL: intuition: `Clause` abbreviates `Vector Literal 3`, so its arity is fixed by the type rather than checked at runtime.
-- L4DEL: step: Positions `0`, `1`, and `2` are all available by finite indexing.
-- L4DEL: step: The vector may repeat a literal or variable name; this alias imposes no distinctness condition.
-- L4DEL: step: Being an abbreviation, `Clause` unfolds directly to the vector type in later definitions.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L51

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryFormula.Clause.eval
-- L4DEL: title: Evaluate a 3SAT clause by disjunction
-- L4DEL: intuition: A clause is true when at least one of its three signed literals evaluates to true under the assignment.
-- L4DEL: step: Evaluate positions zero and one and combine them with Boolean `||`.
-- L4DEL: step: Disjoin that result with the evaluation at position two.
-- L4DEL: step: This is ordinary three-literal OR; no exactly-one or parity interpretation is introduced.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L53-L54

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryFormula.Formula.Satisfiable
-- L4DEL: title: One assignment satisfies every listed clause
-- L4DEL: intuition: `F.Satisfiable` means there exists a total Boolean assignment making each clause occurring in `F.clauses` evaluate to `true`.
-- L4DEL: step: The existential assignment maps every natural-number variable name to a Boolean value.
-- L4DEL: step: The universal condition ranges over list membership, so repeated clauses create repeated obligations with the same truth value.
-- L4DEL: step: An empty formula is satisfiable because the all-clauses condition is vacuous.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L61-L62

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryEncoding.bitsValue
-- L4DEL: title: Interpret a least-significant-bit-first Boolean list
-- L4DEL: intuition: `bitsValue` converts Lean's canonical Boolean digit list back to a natural number.
-- L4DEL: step: The empty list denotes zero.
-- L4DEL: step: A head bit `b` and recursive tail value `n` become `Nat.bit b n`, placing `b` at the low-order end.
-- L4DEL: step: `parseName` later checks the decoded number's `Nat.bits` against the original digits, which supplies canonicality rather than this fold alone.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L70-L72

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryEncoding.parseFrame
-- L4DEL: title: Parse one self-delimiting frame of bits
-- L4DEL: intuition: `parseFrame` reads zero or more data bits, each introduced by `true`, until a `false` terminator, then returns the digits and untouched suffix.
-- L4DEL: step: A leading `false` means the framed digit list is empty.
-- L4DEL: step: A leading `true` requires one following data bit and then recursively parses the rest of the frame.
-- L4DEL: step: End of input before a terminator, or `true` without a following bit, returns `none`.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L74-L79

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryEncoding.parseLiteral
-- L4DEL: title: Parse a sign bit followed by one canonical name
-- L4DEL: intuition: `parseLiteral` consumes the first bit as polarity, delegates the remaining input to `parseName`, and returns one `Literal` plus the suffix.
-- L4DEL: step: With a leading bit `sign`, successful name parsing yields `⟨name, sign⟩`.
-- L4DEL: step: Failure of the nested canonical-name parser propagates through the `Option` computation.
-- L4DEL: step: Empty input fails because a literal must contain a sign even before its framed name.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L87-L91

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryEncoding.parseClause
-- L4DEL: title: Parse three literals in sequence
-- L4DEL: intuition: `parseClause` runs `parseLiteral` three times and packages the results as the length-three vector `#v[a,b,c]`.
-- L4DEL: step: Each successful parser passes its unconsumed suffix to the next literal parser.
-- L4DEL: step: Failure at any of the three positions makes the whole `Option` computation fail.
-- L4DEL: step: No delimiter is needed between literals because each variable name carries its own frame terminator.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L93-L97

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryEncoding.decodeFormula
-- L4DEL: title: Accept exactly one complete canonical formula encoding
-- L4DEL: intuition: `decodeFormula input` parses the clause list with enough fuel and succeeds only when no trailing bits remain.
-- L4DEL: step: The fuel is `input.length + 1`, so recursive clause parsing cannot exhaust it before consuming a finite input.
-- L4DEL: step: `parseClauses` returns both clauses and a suffix; `decodeFormula` rejects every nonempty suffix.
-- L4DEL: step: On exact consumption it wraps the clause list in `Formula`; malformed, unterminated, noncanonical, or trailing encodings return `none`.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L109-L111

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.BinaryLanguage.language
-- L4DEL: title: The encoded language of satisfiable binary 3SAT formulas
-- L4DEL: intuition: `language input` holds exactly when the entire bit list decodes to some formula and that formula has a satisfying Boolean assignment.
-- L4DEL: step: The existential formula is tied to the input by `decodeFormula input = some formula`.
-- L4DEL: step: The second conjunct applies `Formula.Satisfiable` to that same decoded syntax tree.
-- L4DEL: step: Parser failure, trailing bits, or a well-formed unsatisfiable formula all place the input outside the language, for different reasons.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L115-L120
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 102 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/102.md

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Integration.BinaryLinear.F2
-- L4DEL: title: The two-element coefficient field
-- L4DEL: intuition: `F2` abbreviates `ZMod 2`, the field whose two elements encode binary addition and translation.
-- L4DEL: step: Arithmetic is modulo two, so every element is its own additive inverse.
-- L4DEL: step: The abbreviation introduces no new structure or proof; it shortens the types used by the translation contract.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the scalar field for the label coordinate space, not the Boolean type used by formula encodings and graph sides.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L124-L126

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Integration.BinaryLinear.Vector
-- L4DEL: title: A finite binary coordinate vector
-- L4DEL: intuition: `Vector n` abbreviates the function type `Fin n → F2`, so a vector is one modulo-two coordinate for each of `n` positions.
-- L4DEL: step: Pointwise addition supplies the translation operation used in every constraint.
-- L4DEL: step: `Fin n` makes the dimension part of the type and rules out out-of-range coordinates.
-- L4DEL: step: When `n` is positive this type has `2^n` elements; the reduction record separately identifies it with the finite label type.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L128

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Integration.TranslationTarget.IsTranslationInstance
-- L4DEL: title: Every permutation constraint is addition by one binary shift
-- L4DEL: intuition: After identifying labels with a binary vector space, `IsTranslationInstance coordinates g` requires each listed permutation to act as `x ↦ x+shift` for some shift depending on that constraint.
-- L4DEL: step: The property quantifies over every constraint occurrence in `g.constraints`.
-- L4DEL: step: For one occurrence it chooses one vector `shift`, then requires the coordinate equation for every possible label.
-- L4DEL: step: The same coordinate equivalence is fixed for the whole game; only the additive shift may vary between constraints.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L132-L141
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI, The Unique Games Theorem (2026), p. 2 = every constraint is a translation

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Complexity.encodeWord
-- L4DEL: title: Encode one natural number in unary with a zero terminator
-- L4DEL: intuition: `encodeWord n` is `n` copies of `true` followed by one `false`.
-- L4DEL: step: The count of leading true bits recovers the natural number.
-- L4DEL: step: The false terminator makes concatenations of several words uniquely separable.
-- L4DEL: step: This output representation is unary, unlike the canonical binary variable-name framing used for the input formula and the logarithmic endpoint encoding discussed in the manuscript.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L145-L147

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Complexity.encodeWords
-- L4DEL: title: Concatenate a list of unary natural-number words
-- L4DEL: intuition: `encodeWords` maps each natural to `encodeWord` and joins the framed words in list order.
-- L4DEL: step: An empty word list becomes an empty bit list.
-- L4DEL: step: For `n :: ns`, output the frame for `n` followed by the recursive encoding of `ns`.
-- L4DEL: step: Because each word ends in `false`, adjacent frames remain decodable without a separate length field.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L149-L151

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Complexity.tableWords
-- L4DEL: title: Serialize the forward half of a permutation table
-- L4DEL: intuition: `tableWords table` lists the natural values of `table.images` in label order.
-- L4DEL: step: `toList` preserves the vector's `alphabet` entries.
-- L4DEL: step: Mapping `Fin.val` removes the bounded-index wrapper and produces natural-number words.
-- L4DEL: step: The inverse table and inverse-law proofs are omitted because the certified forward permutation determines them mathematically.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L155-L160

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Complexity.constraintWords
-- L4DEL: title: Serialize one constraint as endpoints plus a permutation table
-- L4DEL: intuition: `constraintWords` emits the source index, target index, and every forward permutation image as natural numbers.
-- L4DEL: step: The first two words fix the oriented endpoints.
-- L4DEL: step: `tableWords` appends exactly one image for each alphabet label.
-- L4DEL: step: The alphabet size is fixed outside the input, so each constraint contributes a fixed number of natural-number fields once the error parameters are fixed.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L162-L163

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Complexity.gameWords
-- L4DEL: title: Flatten an explicit game into natural-number fields
-- L4DEL: intuition: `gameWords game` places a three-word header before the serialization of every constraint occurrence.
-- L4DEL: step: The header records the vertex count, fixed alphabet size, and number of list occurrences.
-- L4DEL: step: `flatMap constraintWords` preserves occurrence order and multiplicity while concatenating endpoints and forward tables.
-- L4DEL: step: The explicit occurrence count and fixed table width delimit the remaining fields without serializing proofs or inverse tables.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L165-L166

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.Foundations.Complexity.gameBits
-- L4DEL: title: The comparator's concrete output bit string
-- L4DEL: intuition: `gameBits game` applies the unary `encodeWords` representation to the complete natural-number field list from `gameWords`.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the exact output function named in `BinaryGapReduction.computation`, so the polynomial-time machine contract concerns these bytes, not an unspecified abstract graph.
-- L4DEL: step: The representation is explicit and prefix-decodable, but it uses unary natural-number words. It is not the logarithmic endpoint encoding described in the paper's polynomial-encoding paragraph.
-- L4DEL: step: The theorem's formal contract therefore stands on the Lean proof that this concrete output function is computed in polynomial time; the manuscript is not cited as specifying this encoding.
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L165-L168
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 102 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/102.md

@file ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean
@decl OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.theorem11
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: A polynomial-time binary-3SAT reduction proving the Unique Games gap
-- L4DEL: intuition: For any fixed completeness error ε and soundness bound δ between 0 and 1/2, there exists a concrete polynomial-time reduction from encoded binary 3SAT to a nonempty simple bipartite Unique Games instance. Satisfiable inputs admit a labeling of value at least 1-ε; unsatisfiable inputs give every labeling value at most δ.
-- L4DEL: step: `BinaryGapReduction ε δ` fixes an alphabet of size at least two, a positive dimension, and an identification of its labels with a finite vector space over F₂ before quantifying over inputs.
-- L4DEL: step: Its output instances are nonempty, unweighted, simple and bipartite; every constraint permutation is translation by a fixed vector in those coordinates, and the encoded output is computed by a finite-alphabet Turing machine in polynomial time.
-- L4DEL: step: The Lean conclusion is `Nonempty (BinaryGapReduction ε δ)`. This declaration concerns only the Unique Games theorem, not the four separate Max-Cut, Vertex Cover, Min-UnCut, and directed-feedback results documented in the same note; its challenge proof is `sorry` and the paired solution is `OAI.Computability.UniqueGames.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: Unique Game = a constraint-satisfaction instance in which each edge constraint is a permutation, so either endpoint's label uniquely determines the satisfying label at the other endpoint
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI Lean source for Unique Games = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/ComparatorChallenges/UniqueGamesTheorem.lean#L228-L235
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Unique Games manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Unique-Games-Theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 102 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/102.md
-- L4DEL: source: Subhash Khot, On the Unique Games Conjecture (2010), p. 5 = https://doi.org/10.1109/CCC.2010.19
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Unique Games manuscript (abstract) = We prove the Unique Games Conjecture.
-- L4DEL: quote: Subhash Khot, On the Unique Games Conjecture (2010), p. 5 = The author conjectured that the problem is computationally hard.

@file ComparatorChallenges/QuinticLienard.lean
@decl OAI.QuinticLienard.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Exactly two is the sharp limit-cycle bound for quintic Liénard systems
-- L4DEL: intuition: For the planar system x' = y-F(x), y' = -x, every real polynomial F of degree at most five has at most two geometrically distinct isolated periodic-orbit images, and some such F has exactly two.
-- L4DEL: step: `IsPeriodicOrbit` requires a nonconstant solution z, a positive period T, periodicity with period T, and identifies the orbit C with the range of z.
-- L4DEL: step: `IsLimitCycle` additionally requires an open neighborhood U containing C in which every periodic orbit contained in U has the same image C; the theorem bounds the extended cardinality of this set by two for every degree-≤5 polynomial.
-- L4DEL: step: The second conjunct supplies a degree-≤5 polynomial attaining cardinality two, so the upper bound is sharp. The challenge body is `sorry`; its paired solution module is `OAI.Analysis.LienardCycles.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: limit cycle = the image of a nonconstant periodic solution that is isolated among periodic-orbit images
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI quintic Liénard manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 143 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/143.md
-- L4DEL: source: David Hilbert (1900), Mary Frances Winston Newson translation = https://www.ams.org/bull/1902-08-10/S0002-9904-1902-00923-3/S0002-9904-1902-00923-3.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: A. Lins, W. de Melo, and C. C. Pugh (1977), Liénard conjecture = https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0085364
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI quintic Liénard manuscript (abstract) = has at most two geometrically distinct isolated periodic orbits, and the bound is attained
-- L4DEL: quote: David Hilbert (1900), Mary Frances Winston Newson translation = the maximum number and position of Poincaré's boundary cycles (cycles limites) for a differential equation of the first order

@file ComparatorChallenges/YauCounterexample.lean
@decl OAI.Yau.Target.yau_nodal_set_upper_bound_counterexample
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: A fixed smooth metric with unbounded normalized nodal four-volume
-- L4DEL: intuition: On S⁴×S¹, one fixed smooth Riemannian metric supports nonzero real Laplace eigenfunctions with eigenvalues tending to infinity while four-dimensional nodal measure divided by √λ also tends to infinity. This refutes a uniform O(√λ) upper bound for that metric.
-- L4DEL: step: `MainTarget` existentially fixes one smooth metric g on `Base × Circle`, a positive eigenvalue sequence λ(k), and a sequence of nonzero smooth real functions u(k).
-- L4DEL: step: The eigenfunction equation is written in every extended chart using the coordinate Laplacian built from g, and `nodalMeasure` is the intrinsic four-dimensional Hausdorff measure of the zero set.
-- L4DEL: step: Lean states λ(k) → ∞ and nodalMeasure(g,u(k))/√λ(k) → ∞. The manuscript proves the stronger fixed-power excess λ^(1/2+ε₀); that exponent ε₀ is not present in this target. The challenge body is `sorry`, with paired solution `OAI.Geometry.NodalSets.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: nodal set = the zero set of an eigenfunction
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Yau power-law counterexample manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Power-law-violations-of-Yaus-nodal-upper-bound-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 350 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/350.md
-- L4DEL: source: Shing-Tung Yau (1982), original problem section = https://doi.org/10.1515/9781400881918-035
-- L4DEL: source: Harold Donnelly and Charles Fefferman (1988), analytic-metric theorem = https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01393691
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Yau power-law counterexample manuscript (abstract) = This disproves the smooth upper-bound assertion in Yau’s nodal-set conjecture

@file ComparatorChallenges/SeymourSecondNeighborhood.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.exists_goodVertex
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Seymour's second-neighborhood conjecture
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every nonempty finite oriented graph has a vertex whose second out-neighborhood is at least as large as its first out-neighborhood. Here "second" means directed distance exactly two: the vertex itself and vertices already reached in one step are excluded.
-- L4DEL: step: `IsOriented` requires a loopless asymmetric relation. `firstNeighbors r v` is the set of out-neighbors of `v`; `secondNeighbors r v` contains each vertex reached in two steps only when it is neither `v` nor already a first neighbor.
-- L4DEL: step: `GoodVertex r v` is the cardinal inequality `|N₁⁺(v)| ≤ |N₂⁺(v)|`. The theorem assumes only that the finite vertex type is nonempty and the relation is oriented; it adds no connectivity or minimum-degree hypothesis, so a sink is allowed.
-- L4DEL: step: The manuscript argues by excluding a minimal counterexample, strengthening the deficit to subsets, passing to a graph product, and applying a pruning lemma. Those are proof ideas from the manuscript, not extra hypotheses encoded in this comparator statement. Its body is `sorry`; the paired solution module is `OAI.Combinatorics.SecondNeighborhood.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: oriented graph = a directed graph with no loops and no pair of oppositely directed edges
-- L4DEL: term: second out-neighborhood = vertices at directed distance exactly two, excluding the starting vertex and its first out-neighbors
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 173 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/173.md
-- L4DEL: source: David C. Fisher (1996), tournament special case, abstract = https://doi.org/10.1002/(SICI)1097-0118(199609)23:1%3C43::AID-JGT4%3E3.0.CO;2-K
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = every nonempty finite oriented graph has a vertex with at least as many vertices at directed distance two as at directed distance one
-- L4DEL: quote: David C. Fisher (1996), tournament special case, abstract = any tournament has a node whose outdegree is at least doubled in its square

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/Model.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.IsOriented
-- L4DEL: title: The loopless, asymmetric relation underlying an oriented graph
-- L4DEL: intuition: `IsOriented r` packages the two relation-level conditions needed throughout the development: no vertex points to itself, and an arc `u → v` rules out the opposite arc `v → u`. The Lean model uses a relation rather than a graph structure, so finiteness and decidable equality are supplied separately where needed.
-- L4DEL: step: `loopless` excludes `r v v` for every vertex.
-- L4DEL: step: `asymmetric` excludes a two-cycle formed by opposite arcs; it is stronger than merely having no duplicate representation of an arc.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = An oriented graph is a finite directed graph with no loops, no multiple arcs, and no pair of opposite arcs.

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/Model.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.firstNeighbors
-- L4DEL: title: The first out-neighborhood of a vertex
-- L4DEL: intuition: `firstNeighbors r v` is the finite set of vertices `u` with an outgoing arc `r v u`. It is the manuscript's `N₁⁺(v)`, counting each reached vertex once.
-- L4DEL: step: The definition filters the finite universe by the predicate `r v`.
-- L4DEL: step: It records endpoints, not arcs or paths, so parallel witnesses would not change the cardinality even in the relational presentation.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = N1+ (v) = {u : v → u}

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/Model.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.secondNeighbors
-- L4DEL: title: Vertices at directed distance exactly two
-- L4DEL: intuition: `secondNeighbors r v` contains a vertex `w` when some `u` gives a two-step path `v → u → w`, while explicitly excluding `v` itself and every first out-neighbor of `v`. This is exact distance two, not merely reachability within two steps.
-- L4DEL: step: The existential witness supplies a directed walk of length two.
-- L4DEL: step: The two negative conditions remove the starting vertex and all vertices already reached in one step; the surrounding `Finset` deduplicates multiple paths to the same endpoint.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = Thus N2+ (v) consists of the vertices at directed distance exactly two from v.

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/Model.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.GoodVertex
-- L4DEL: title: A vertex with at least as many second as first out-neighbors
-- L4DEL: intuition: `GoodVertex r v` is precisely the conjectured cardinal inequality `|N₁⁺(v)| ≤ |N₂⁺(v)|`. It does not require either neighborhood to be nonempty, so every sink is automatically good.
-- L4DEL: step: Both sides count distinct vertices using the two finite-neighborhood definitions.
-- L4DEL: step: The global theorem asks for the existence of at least one vertex satisfying this local predicate.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = Seymour’s second neighborhood conjecture asserts that some vertex satisfies

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/ReductionCore.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.Counterexample
-- L4DEL: title: Every vertex has a strict first-neighborhood surplus
-- L4DEL: intuition: `Counterexample r` says that every vertex fails `GoodVertex`: its exact second out-neighborhood is strictly smaller than its first. This pointwise strict inequality is the negation normal form used by the minimal-counterexample argument.
-- L4DEL: step: Because the graph is finite, negating `|N₁⁺| ≤ |N₂⁺|` becomes `|N₂⁺| < |N₁⁺|`.
-- L4DEL: step: The definition alone does not assert orientation or nonemptiness; those hypotheses are carried separately in the reduction theorems.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = |N1+ (v)| > |N2+ (v)| for every v ∈ V (D).

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/ReductionCore.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.PositiveIndegree
-- L4DEL: title: Every vertex has an incoming neighbor
-- L4DEL: intuition: `PositiveIndegree r` requires an incoming witness `u → v` for each vertex `v`. In the reduction this property follows from strong connectivity of a minimal counterexample and survives the transitive-tournament blowup.
-- L4DEL: step: The property is existential at each vertex rather than a numerical degree bound.
-- L4DEL: step: Later it ensures the image of the full vertex set is again the full set and prevents degenerate rows or columns in the extremal construction.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = Every vertex of G has positive indegree.

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/ReductionCore.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.StrictSubsetGrowth
-- L4DEL: title: Every nonempty proper subset has a strict one-step image surplus
-- L4DEL: intuition: `StrictSubsetGrowth r` is the amplified inequality `|S|+|F²(S)| < 2|F(S)|` for every nonempty proper finite subset. It compares ordinary relation images, which may overlap `S`, rather than exact-distance neighborhoods.
-- L4DEL: step: The left side combines the starting set with its twice-iterated image.
-- L4DEL: step: The strict factor-two inequality is the obstruction later ruled out by the extremal pruning argument.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = |U | + |FG2 (U )| < 2|FG (U )|

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/MinimalCounterexample.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.exists_minimal_counterexample
-- L4DEL: title: Select a smallest counterexample and then minimize its arcs
-- L4DEL: intuition: From any finite oriented counterexample, this theorem reindexes a counterexample onto `Fin n`, minimizes `n`, then minimizes the number of arcs. The chosen relation is oriented, remains a counterexample, is order- and arc-minimal, has nonempty boundary for every nontrivial subset, and has positive indegree.
-- L4DEL: step: `Nat.find` first selects the least positive order admitting an oriented counterexample.
-- L4DEL: step: A second well-founded choice selects the least arc count at that order; previously proved minimality consequences supply boundary nonemptiness and incoming neighbors.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = choose one with minimum order and then minimum number of arcs.

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/SubsetDeficit.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.subsetDeficit_of_minimal
-- L4DEL: title: Minimal counterexamples satisfy the strengthened subset deficit
-- L4DEL: intuition: In an oriented counterexample minimal by arcs and equipped with a nonempty boundary, every nonempty proper set `S` has fewer newly reached second-image vertices than first-image vertices lying outside `S`. This strengthens the earlier subset perspective by using `|F(S)\S|` on the right.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof repeatedly augments a subset of the boundary while an arc-deletion argument makes the deficit potential grow by less than the number of added vertices.
-- L4DEL: step: At the full boundary, the terminal potential is exactly `|F²(S)\F(S)|`, yielding the strict subset inequality.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Tyler Seacrest, Seymour's Second Neighborhood Conjecture for Subsets of Vertices (2019) = https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.06293v3
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = Minimality first gives a strict deficit for subsets of a counterexample.
-- L4DEL: quote: Tyler Seacrest, Seymour's Second Neighborhood Conjecture for Subsets of Vertices (2019) = The following lemma says, starting with a counterexample to the SNC, one can remove edges

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/TransitiveBlowup.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.blowup_strictSubsetGrowth
-- L4DEL: title: A large transitive-tournament blowup amplifies subset deficit into strict growth
-- L4DEL: intuition: Replace each base vertex by a transitive tournament with block size `m` larger than the number of base vertices. If the base graph has positive indegree and the strengthened subset deficit, the blowup satisfies `|U|+|F²(U)| < 2|F(U)|` for every nonempty proper subset `U`.
-- L4DEL: step: Full-support subsets are handled using positive indegree, which fills every first- and second-image block.
-- L4DEL: step: For proper support, multiplying the integer deficit by `m` dominates the at-most-one internal error per base block; `m > |V|` makes the final bound strict.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: James Brantner, Greg Brockman, Bill Kay, and Emma Snively, Contributions to Seymour's second neighborhood conjecture (2009) = https://doi.org/10.2140/involve.2009.2.387
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = Replacing each vertex by a sufficiently large transitive tournament then turns that deficit into (2).
-- L4DEL: quote: James Brantner, Greg Brockman, Bill Kay, and Emma Snively, Contributions to Seymour's second neighborhood conjecture (2009) = there are infinitely many non-isomorphic strongly-connected counterexamples

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/Reduction.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.counterexample_reduction
-- L4DEL: title: Reduce any counterexample to a positive-indegree strict-growth graph
-- L4DEL: intuition: A hypothetical oriented counterexample yields a finite nonempty oriented graph `g` with positive indegree and `StrictSubsetGrowth g`. The theorem composes the minimal-counterexample selection, strengthened subset deficit, and a transitive-tournament blowup with block size `n+1`.
-- L4DEL: step: Minimality supplies a relation on `Fin n` with the arc and boundary hypotheses needed for the subset-deficit theorem.
-- L4DEL: step: Blowing it up by `n+1` preserves orientation and positive indegree while turning the deficit into the strict factor-two image inequality.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = There are two steps. Minimality first gives a strict deficit for subsets of a counterexample.

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/Pruning.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.Pruning.pruning
-- L4DEL: title: Prune two pair families to compatibility with a controlled deletion cost
-- L4DEL: intuition: For any finite binary relation and two families of ordered pairs, the pruning theorem retains every member that was conflict-free initially and removes enough other members to eliminate all conflicts. The total deletions are charged to original conflict corners plus conflict corners left uncovered afterward.
-- L4DEL: step: An augmented bipartite matching represents the possible conflicting deletions.
-- L4DEL: step: Maximum-support matrix ranks bound that matching, and a matching-to-cover argument converts the bound into compatible retained subfamilies satisfying `PruningStatement`.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = There are subfamilies R′ ⊆ R and C ′ ⊆ C with no conflicts between them

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/ExtremalContradiction.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.Extremal.no_strictSubsetGrowth_of_pruning
-- L4DEL: title: Pruning contradicts the existence of a positive-indegree strict-growth graph
-- L4DEL: intuition: No nonempty finite oriented graph can simultaneously have positive indegree, satisfy the pruning statement, and obey `StrictSubsetGrowth`. An extremal compatible pair of ordered-pair families can always be replaced by a strictly better one, contradicting maximality.
-- L4DEL: step: Complementing second iterated coordinate images creates candidate families whose combined size beats the current objective by more than twice the uncovered count.
-- L4DEL: step: Pruning restores compatibility while charging deletions to old and new uncovered points; the latter cancel in the objective, leaving a strict improvement.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = There is no nonempty finite oriented graph G in which every vertex has an in-neighbor

@file OAI/Combinatorics/SecondNeighborhood/Main.lean
@decl OAI.SeymourSecondNeighborhood.exists_goodVertex
-- L4DEL: title: Proof-bearing solution of Seymour's second-neighborhood conjecture
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every nonempty finite oriented graph has a good vertex. Unlike the comparator declaration with the same fully qualified name, this declaration imports the reduction, pruning theorem, and extremal contradiction and contains the checked proof term that composes them.
-- L4DEL: step: Assuming no good vertex produces `Counterexample r`, which `counterexample_reduction` converts into an oriented positive-indegree graph with strict subset growth.
-- L4DEL: step: The general pruning theorem applies to that graph, while the extremal theorem proves pruning and strict subset growth incompatible; the contradiction discharges the original assumption.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-proof-of-Seymours-second-neighborhood-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 173 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/173.md
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Seymour second-neighborhood manuscript = every nonempty finite oriented graph has a vertex with at least as many vertices at directed distance two as at directed distance one

@file ComparatorChallenges/CannonGeometricAction.lean
@decl OAI.CannonRelease.cannon
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Cannon's conjecture
-- L4DEL: intuition: A hyperbolic group whose boundary is a topological two-sphere admits an isometric action on hyperbolic three-space that is proper and cocompact and has finite kernel.
-- L4DEL: step: `CayleyData` chooses a finite symmetric generating set whose Cayley graph is connected. `ThinTriangles` supplies one uniform natural-number bound for the distance from every point on one geodesic side to the other two sides.
-- L4DEL: step: The boundary is a quotient of based integer-time geodesic rays by bounded synchronous distance. The hypothesis `Nonempty (Boundary D ≃ₜ SphereTwo)` records the boundary homeomorphism to S².
-- L4DEL: step: The conclusion produces a homomorphism into the full isometry group of the upper-half-space model of H³. `ProperAction`, `CocompactAction`, and finite kernel are three separate conjuncts; orientation preservation is not required.
-- L4DEL: step: The manuscript's route is an upper combinatorial-modulus bound, boundary uniformization, and straightening of the boundary action. This comparator body is `sorry`; the paired solution module is `OAI.Geometry.Cannon.Main`, so the prose does not substitute for its Lean proof.
-- L4DEL: term: geometric action = an isometric group action that is proper and cocompact
-- L4DEL: term: hyperbolic-group boundary = asymptotic classes of geodesic rays in a Cayley graph, equipped with its boundary topology
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Cannon manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 246 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/246.md
-- L4DEL: source: James W. Cannon and Eric L. Swenson (1998), abstract = https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-98-02107-2
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Cannon manuscript (abstract) = every hyperbolic group whose boundary is homeomorphic to the two-sphere admits a proper cocompact isometric action on hyperbolic three-space with finite kernel
-- L4DEL: quote: James W. Cannon and Eric L. Swenson (1998), abstract = We characterize those discrete groups G which can act properly discontinuously, isometrically, and cocompactly on hyperbolic 3-space H3

@file ComparatorChallenges/KoebeCircleDomains.lean
@decl OAI.Problem047.koebe_circle_domain
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Koebe's unrestricted circle-domain uniformization
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every nonempty connected open subset of the Riemann sphere is conformally equivalent to a circle domain, with no countability restriction on its complementary components.
-- L4DEL: step: The input `U` is any open connected set in the sphere. The output is a set `V` and a sphere map `f` such that `V` is a circle domain and `f` is a conformal equivalence from `U` to `V`.
-- L4DEL: step: `IsCircleDomain V` says that `V` is open and connected and that each connected component of its complement is either a singleton or the Möbius image of a closed round unit disk.
-- L4DEL: step: He and Schramm proved the countably connected case in 1993. The pinned manuscript states the unrestricted result; this distinction matters because the Lean theorem contains no countability assumption. Its comparator body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.Analysis.CircleDomains.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: circle domain = a domain in the Riemann sphere whose complementary components are round closed disks or single points
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Koebe circle-domain manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Koebes-Circle-Domain-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 071 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/071.md
-- L4DEL: source: Zheng-Xu He and Oded Schramm (1993), Theorem 0.1 = https://doi.org/10.2307/2946541
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Koebe circle-domain manuscript (abstract) = every domain in the Riemann sphere is conformally equivalent to a circle domain
-- L4DEL: quote: Zheng-Xu He and Oded Schramm (1993), Theorem 0.1 = Any domain Q in C, whose boundary has at most countably many components, is conformally homeomorphic to a circle domain

@file ComparatorChallenges/KoebeCircleDomains.lean
@decl OAI.Problem047.removability_implies_rigidity
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Conformal removability implies circle-domain rigidity
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the boundary of a circle domain is conformally removable, then every conformal equivalence from it to another circle domain is the restriction of a Möbius transformation, regardless of how many complementary components there are.
-- L4DEL: step: The theorem separately assumes that `U` and `V` are circle domains, that `frontier U` is conformally removable, and that `f` is a conformal equivalence from `U` to `V`.
-- L4DEL: step: `IsConformallyRemovable` quantifies over orientation-preserving sphere homeomorphisms conformal off the compact set. The conclusion supplies one Möbius transformation `M` agreeing with `f` at every point of `U`.
-- L4DEL: step: This is only the removability-to-rigidity direction associated with the He–Schramm program; it is not an equivalence and it does not claim the converse. The body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.Analysis.CircleDomains.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: conformally removable = a compact set outside which every orientation-preserving conformal sphere homeomorphism must already be Möbius everywhere
-- L4DEL: term: conformally rigid circle domain = a circle domain whose conformal equivalences to other circle domains are restrictions of Möbius transformations
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI circle-domain rigidity manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Removable-Boundaries-and-Rigidity-of-Circle-Domains-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 071 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/071.md
-- L4DEL: source: Malik Younsi (2015), arXiv v1 abstract = https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.07348v1
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI circle-domain rigidity manuscript (abstract) = a circle domain with conformally removable boundary is conformally rigid, with no restriction on the number of complementary components
-- L4DEL: quote: Malik Younsi (2015), arXiv v1 abstract = two rigidity conjectures of He and Schramm are in fact equivalent, at least for a large family of circle domains

@file ComparatorChallenges/ThreeTorus.lean
@decl OAI.ThreeTorus.simple_lebesgue_spectrum
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: A smooth probability-preserving system with simple Lebesgue spectrum
-- L4DEL: intuition: There is a smooth volume-preserving diffeomorphism of the three-torus whose Koopman operator has simple Lebesgue spectrum on the entire complex mean-zero L² space. One real-valued vector generates a bilateral orthonormal basis under all integer iterates.
-- L4DEL: step: The probability space is the three-torus `(R/Z)³` with normalized product Haar measure. `H₀` is the complex L² subspace of functions with integral zero, removing the unavoidable invariant constants.
-- L4DEL: step: `MainConclusion` supplies a torus permutation `T` whose map and inverse are locally C∞ lifts, which preserves volume, together with a real-valued `f` and a sequence `v(n)=f∘Tⁿ` forming a dense orthonormal spanning family.
-- L4DEL: step: The same conclusion states ergodicity and gives a complex-linear isometric equivalence `W` to circle L² under which composition by `T` becomes multiplication by the first Fourier character. This is the precise simple-Lebesgue spectral model.
-- L4DEL: step: Earlier work cited here includes an infinite-measure solution; this target is the stronger smooth compact probability-preserving form and should not be conflated with it. The comparator body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.Dynamics.ThreeTorus.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: Koopman operator = the operator on functions given here by composition with the measure-preserving transformation
-- L4DEL: term: simple Lebesgue spectrum = unitary equivalence to one copy of multiplication by the circle coordinate, rather than multiple spectral copies
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI simple-Lebesgue-spectrum manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-smooth-three-torus-diffeomorphism-with-simple-Lebesgue-spectrum-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 144 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/144.md
-- L4DEL: source: el Houcein el Abdalaoui (2015), arXiv v1 abstract = https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.06439v1
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI simple-Lebesgue-spectrum manuscript (abstract) = a C∞ diffeomorphism of the three-torus that preserves standard volume and whose Koopman operator has simple Lebesgue spectrum
-- L4DEL: quote: el Houcein el Abdalaoui (2015), arXiv v1 abstract = there is a conservative, ergodic, σ-finite measure preserving transformation with simple Lebesgue spectrum

@file ComparatorChallenges/Rokhlin.lean
@decl OAI.Rokhlin.mixing_all_finite_orders
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Ordinary mixing implies mixing of every finite order
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every invertible probability-preserving transformation that is mixing for pairs of measurable sets is mixing for every finite number of sets when all successive time gaps diverge.
-- L4DEL: step: `IsMixing` says `μ(A ∩ T⁻ⁿB)` tends to `μ(A)μ(B)` as the absolute integer time tends to infinity, for every measurable pair `A,B`.
-- L4DEL: step: `layoutTime` builds ordered observation times from positive gaps. `MixingOfOrder k` asks the measure of the intersection of all `k` translated sets to converge to the product of their measures as the entire gap vector tends to infinity.
-- L4DEL: step: The theorem assumes an arbitrary probability measure, an invertible measurable equivalence preserving that measure, and ordinary mixing. It concludes `MixingOfOrder k` for every `k≥3`; no standardness or countable-generation hypothesis appears in the Lean statement.
-- L4DEL: step: De la Rue's 2006 paper records the twofold-to-threefold question as still open at that time. The comparator body here is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.Dynamics.MultipleMixing.Main`; the linked manuscript supplies the claimed affirmative proof exposition.
-- L4DEL: term: mixing of order k = asymptotic factorization of every k-fold translated intersection when all consecutive time gaps diverge
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Rokhlin multiple-mixing manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Rokhlins-multiple-mixing-problem-for-one-transformation-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 145 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/145.md
-- L4DEL: source: Thierry de la Rue (2006), arXiv v1 abstract = https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0603154v1
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Rokhlin multiple-mixing manuscript (abstract) = Every invertible mixing probability-preserving transformation is mixing of every finite order
-- L4DEL: quote: Thierry de la Rue (2006), arXiv v1 abstract = Rohlin asked in 1949 whether 2-fold mixing implies 3-fold mixing for a stationary process indexed by Z

@file ComparatorChallenges/BorsukNine.lean
@decl OAI.BorsukNine.main_theorem
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Borsuk's covering assertion fails in dimension nine
-- L4DEL: intuition: The compact set of rank-one orthogonal projectors onto lines in R⁴ lies in a nine-dimensional affine Euclidean space, has diameter √2, and cannot be covered by ten subsets of smaller diameter.
-- L4DEL: step: `projector u` is the matrix `u uᵀ`. `projectorSet` is its image over unit vectors, and `traceOneSymmetric` is the affine set of symmetric 4×4 matrices with trace one.
-- L4DEL: step: The theorem proves compactness, containment in the trace-one symmetric affine space, and exact Frobenius diameter `√2` as separate conjuncts.
-- L4DEL: step: `HasTenSmallCover` allows any ten subsets of the projector set, not necessarily open, closed, or measurable. Its negation says no such family both covers the set and has every diameter strictly below `√2`.
-- L4DEL: step: Kahn and Kalai's 1993 counterexamples established failure in sufficiently high dimensions. The pinned manuscript claims dimension nine via the full compact projector image. This comparator body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.Geometry.Borsuk.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: Frobenius metric = Euclidean distance between matrices obtained from the square root of the sum of squared entries
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI nine-dimensional Borsuk manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-nine-dimensional-counterexample-to-Borsuks-covering-assertion-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 156 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/156.md
-- L4DEL: source: Jeff Kahn and Gil Kalai (1993), arXiv v1 abstract = https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9307229v1
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI nine-dimensional Borsuk manuscript (abstract) = cannot be covered by ten sets of strictly smaller diameter
-- L4DEL: quote: Jeff Kahn and Gil Kalai (1993), arXiv v1 abstract = Borsuk's conjecture was that f(d)=d+1. We prove that f(d)≥(1.2)^√d for large d

@file ComparatorChallenges/TingleySphereIsometry.lean
@decl OAI.Tingley.tingley_sphere_isometry_full
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Every surjective Banach-sphere isometry extends uniquely and linearly
-- L4DEL: intuition: A surjective isometry between the unit spheres of arbitrary nonzero real Banach spaces extends uniquely to a surjective real-linear isometric equivalence of the whole spaces.
-- L4DEL: step: The only structural hypotheses on `X` and `Y` are complete real normed additive groups and nontriviality. The statement assumes no finite dimension, separability, reflexivity, smoothness, or strict convexity.
-- L4DEL: step: The resulting `T : X ≃ₗᵢ[R] Y` agrees with `f` on the unit sphere, sends zero to zero, and obeys the forced radial formula `T x = ‖x‖ • f(x/‖x‖)` for every nonzero `x`.
-- L4DEL: step: The last conjunct proves uniqueness among all real-linear extensions agreeing with `f` on the sphere. For complex Banach spaces regarded as real spaces, this establishes real linearity only; complex linearity is not claimed.
-- L4DEL: step: Tingley's 1987 paper proved antipode preservation in finite dimensions. The pinned manuscript claims the unrestricted extension theorem. This comparator body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.Analysis.SphereIsometry.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: unit sphere = the vectors of norm exactly one in a normed space
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Tingley manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 322 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/322.md
-- L4DEL: source: Daryl Tingley (1987), original-paper abstract = https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00147942
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Tingley manuscript (abstract) = Every surjective isometry between the unit spheres of real Banach spaces extends uniquely to a surjective real-linear isometry
-- L4DEL: quote: Daryl Tingley (1987), original-paper abstract = isometries between the unit spheres of finite dimensional Banach spaces necessarily map antipodal points to antipodal points

@file ComparatorChallenges/AbhyankarSathaye.lean
@decl OAI.AbhyankarSathaye.exists_noncoordinate_polynomial
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Affine-space zero fibre without an ambient coordinate
-- L4DEL: intuition: In every complex ambient dimension at least four, there is a polynomial whose zero fibre is affine space of one lower dimension even though no polynomial change of coordinates turns that polynomial into a coordinate variable.
-- L4DEL: step: The input is a natural number `n` with `4 ≤ n`. The conclusion chooses a polynomial `F` in the `n`-variable complex polynomial ring.
-- L4DEL: step: The first conjunct supplies a complex-algebra equivalence from the quotient by the principal ideal generated by `F` to a polynomial ring in `n-1` variables. Algebraically, the hypersurface cut out by `F` has the coordinate ring of affine `(n-1)`-space.
-- L4DEL: step: The second conjunct rules out every complex-algebra automorphism of the ambient polynomial ring and every coordinate index whose coordinate variable is sent to `F`. Thus the quotient looks standard while the embedding is not rectifiable by an ambient polynomial automorphism.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement starts at dimension four and does not claim a three-variable counterexample, classify all fibres, or expose the manuscript's explicit formula. The comparator body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.AlgebraicGeometry.AbhyankarSathaye.Counterexample`.
-- L4DEL: term: coordinate polynomial = a polynomial that is the image of a coordinate variable under an automorphism of the ambient polynomial ring
-- L4DEL: term: zero fibre = the hypersurface obtained by imposing the equation `F = 0`, represented algebraically by quotienting by the ideal generated by `F`
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Abhyankar-Sathaye counterexample manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/An-explicit-noncoordinate-polynomial-with-affine-three-space-zero-fibre-September-24-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 049 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/049.md
-- L4DEL: source: Avinash Sathaye (1976), original paper = https://www.ms.uky.edu/~sohum/ma561/affgeom/linear_planes.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Abhyankar-Sathaye counterexample manuscript (abstract) = a noncoordinate polynomial in four complex variables whose zero fibre is affine three-space
-- L4DEL: quote: Avinash Sathaye (1976), original paper = we show that by an automorphism of the affine 3-space, it can be transformed to a coordinate plane

@file ComparatorChallenges/CirculantHadamard.lean
@decl OAI.CirculantHadamard.exists_iff_order_one_or_four
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Real circulant Hadamard matrices have order one or four
-- L4DEL: intuition: A positive-order real matrix whose entries are signs, whose rows are cyclic shifts, and whose rows are mutually orthogonal exists exactly in orders one and four.
-- L4DEL: step: `IsCirculant H` means that one function `h` supplies every entry as `H i j = h (j-i)`, so each row is a cyclic shift of the same sign pattern.
-- L4DEL: step: `IsSignHadamard H` requires every entry to equal `1` or `-1` and requires `H * Hᵀ = nI`. `ExistsRealCirculantHadamard n` existentially combines these conditions.
-- L4DEL: step: For every positive `n`, the theorem proves an equivalence: such a matrix exists if and only if `n=1` or `n=4`. Both existence directions and every nonexistence case are part of the same statement.
-- L4DEL: step: This declaration concerns real sign-valued circulant Hadamard matrices only. The Barker-sequence corollary discussed by the manuscript is a separate comparator statement. The body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.LinearAlgebra.CirculantHadamard.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: circulant matrix = a matrix in which every row is a cyclic shift of one common row
-- L4DEL: term: Hadamard matrix = here, a sign-valued square matrix whose product with its transpose is its order times the identity
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI circulant-Hadamard manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-circulant-Hadamard-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 179 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/179.md
-- L4DEL: source: Richard Turyn and James Storer (1961), original paper = https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9939-1961-0125026-2
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI circulant-Hadamard manuscript (abstract) = a real circulant Hadamard matrix has order 1 or 4
-- L4DEL: quote: Richard Turyn and James Storer (1961), original paper = It will be shown here that for odd n, these are all the sequences with the property |cₖ| ≤ 1

@file ComparatorChallenges/EilenbergGanea.lean
@decl OAI.EilenbergGanea.source_main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: A finitely generated Eilenberg-Ganea counterexample
-- L4DEL: intuition: One explicit height-kernel group is finitely generated and residually finite, has integral cohomological dimension two, admits a three-dimensional classifying space, and admits no two-dimensional classifying space.
-- L4DEL: step: `SourceGroup` is the kernel of the homomorphism sending every generator of a right-angled Artin group to `1`; the Artin group is built from the finite flag-complex data encoded by `seedGraph`.
-- L4DEL: step: The first two conjuncts assert finite generation and residual finiteness. The third identifies the projective dimension of the trivial integral group-ring module as exactly two.
-- L4DEL: step: The final conjuncts give a three-dimensional connected CW classifying space with contractible cover and rule out every two-dimensional one. Together, these say geometric dimension three while cohomological dimension is two.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an existence result for the specifically defined `SourceGroup`, not a statement about all groups of cohomological dimension two. The comparator body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.Topology.EilenbergGanea.Main`.
-- L4DEL: acronym: CW = closure-finite weak topology
-- L4DEL: term: cohomological dimension = the projective dimension of the trivial integral module over the group's integral group ring
-- L4DEL: term: geometric dimension = the least dimension of a classifying CW space for the group
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Eilenberg-Ganea counterexample manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-finitely-generated-counterexample-to-the-Eilenberg-Ganea-conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 249 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/249.md
-- L4DEL: source: Samuel Eilenberg and Tudor Ganea (1957), original paper = https://doi.org/10.2307/1970062
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Eilenberg-Ganea counterexample manuscript (abstract) = a finitely generated residually finite group of integral cohomological dimension two and geometric dimension three
-- L4DEL: quote: Samuel Eilenberg and Tudor Ganea (1957), original paper = The least dimension of such a complex KΠ is called the geometric dimension of Π

@file ComparatorChallenges/ShortEgyptianFractions.lean
@decl OAI.ShortEgyptian.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: The optimal uniform length of Egyptian-fraction expansions
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every proper positive rational number has an expansion into distinct increasing unit fractions, and the worst minimum number of terms among fractions with denominator `b` grows on the order of `log(log b)`.
-- L4DEL: step: `IsExpansion a b ns` requires a strictly increasing list of denominators, every denominator at least two, whose reciprocal sum is exactly `a/b`. The first conjunct supplies such a list for every `1 ≤ a < b`.
-- L4DEL: step: `minLength a b` is the least attainable list length, while `maxMinLength b` takes the finite supremum over numerators `1 ≤ a < b`. This makes the bound uniform in the numerator.
-- L4DEL: step: The second conjunct supplies positive constants `c₁,c₂` and a threshold `b₀` so that every `b ≥ b₀` satisfies `c₁ log(log b) ≤ maxMinLength b ≤ c₂ log(log b)`. This is the precise two-sided asymptotic-order claim.
-- L4DEL: step: The constants and threshold are existential rather than explicit, and this declaration does not include the manuscript's separate counting or prescribed-denominator results. The comparator body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.NumberTheory.ShortEgyptian.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: Egyptian-fraction expansion = a representation of a positive rational number as a finite sum of distinct positive unit fractions
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI short-Egyptian-fractions manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026/Short-Egyptian-fractions-September-25-2026.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 025 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/025.md
-- L4DEL: source: Paul Erdős (1950), original paper = https://www.renyi.hu/~p_erdos/1950-02.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI short-Egyptian-fractions manuscript (abstract) = every rational number a/b with 1 ≤ a < b is a sum of O(log log b) distinct positive unit fractions
-- L4DEL: quote: Paul Erdős (1950), original paper = It seems likely that for 1 < a < b, N(a,b) < c' log log b

@file ComparatorChallenges/CompleteCrouzeix.lean
@decl OAI.CompleteCrouzeix.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: The complete Crouzeix inequality has sharp constant two
-- L4DEL: intuition: Evaluate a matrix-valued polynomial on a complex matrix by replacing each scalar power $z^k$ with the matrix power $A^k$. Its operator norm is at most twice the largest coefficient-matrix norm attained while $z$ ranges over the numerical range of $A$, and no smaller constant works uniformly.
-- L4DEL: step: `numericalRange A` consists of the values $\langle x,Ax\rangle$ for unit vectors $x$. `polynomialValue B z` is $\sum_k z^k B_k$, while `polynomialAt A B` is the block matrix $\sum_k A^k\otimes B_k$.
-- L4DEL: step: `UniversalBound C` quantifies over every positive base-matrix size, every positive coefficient-matrix size, every polynomial degree, every complex matrix $A$, and every matrix coefficient family $B$. It requires $\|P[A]\|\le C\sup_{z\in W(A)}\|P(z)\|$.
-- L4DEL: step: The first conjunct says `2` is a universal bound. The second says every universal bound `C` satisfies `2 ≤ C`, so the constant is optimal as well as sufficient.
-- L4DEL: step: This finite-matrix comparator statement does not itself state the manuscript's Hilbert-space, rational-function, optimal-similarity, or common-boundary-density results. Its body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.Analysis.NumericalRange.Main` by the pinned comparator configuration.
-- L4DEL: term: numerical range = the set of scalar values $\langle x,Ax\rangle$ obtained from unit vectors $x$
-- L4DEL: term: complete Crouzeix inequality = the Crouzeix norm bound required uniformly for matrix-valued, rather than only scalar-valued, polynomials
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI complete-Crouzeix manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-complete-Crouzeix-theorem-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 325 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/325.md
-- L4DEL: source: Michel Crouzeix (2004), original-paper abstract = https://doi.org/10.1007/S00020-002-1188-6
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI complete-Crouzeix manuscript (abstract) = for every bounded operator on a complex Hilbert space and every matrix-valued polynomial
-- L4DEL: quote: Michel Crouzeix (2004), original-paper abstract = we study bounds for ||f(A)|| uniform with respect to the functions f bounded by 1

@file ComparatorChallenges/EuclideanFiveColor.lean
@decl OAI.EuclideanFiveColor.no_proper_five_coloring
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Five colors cannot properly color the Euclidean plane
-- L4DEL: intuition: However five labels are assigned to all points of the plane, some two points exactly one unit apart receive the same label. The coloring is an arbitrary function: measurability, continuity, and region-shaped color classes are not assumed.
-- L4DEL: step: Lean represents the plane as $\mathbb C$. `ProperColoring 5 coloring` says that whenever $\|x-y\|=1$, the values `coloring x` and `coloring y` in `Fin 5` are different.
-- L4DEL: step: The theorem negates the existence of any such function. It therefore gives the six-color lower bound, but it does not contain the separate seven-color construction recorded by formalization note 158.
-- L4DEL: step: De Grey's 2018 finite unit-distance graph first raised the unrestricted lower bound from four to five. The linked OpenAI manuscript claims the next step, excluding five colors even without regularity assumptions. This comparator body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.Geometry.PlaneColoring.Five`.
-- L4DEL: term: Hadwiger-Nelson problem = the problem of finding the smallest number of colors needed so that points of the plane exactly one unit apart always have different colors
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Euclidean-five-color manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Euclidean-plane-is-not-five-colorable-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 158 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/158.md
-- L4DEL: source: Aubrey D. N. J. de Grey (2018), arXiv v3 abstract = https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.02385v3
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Euclidean-five-color manuscript (abstract) = every coloring of the Euclidean plane with five colors has a monochromatic unit-distance pair, with no regularity assumption on the color classes
-- L4DEL: quote: Aubrey D. N. J. de Grey (2018), arXiv v3 abstract = a family of finite unit-distance graphs in the plane that are not 4-colourable

@file ComparatorChallenges/GeneralMahler.lean
@decl OAI.GeneralMahler.general_mahler
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: The general Mahler bound, with equality exactly for simplices
-- L4DEL: intuition: Among all compact convex bodies with interior in $\mathbb R^n$, the smallest possible product of the body's volume and the volume of an optimally centered polar is $(n+1)^{n+1}/(n!)^2$. Exactly the simplices attain it.
-- L4DEL: step: `polarAt K z` contains the vectors $y$ whose pairing with every displacement $x-z$ from the chosen interior center is at most one. `volumeProduct K` is the infimum of $|K|\,|(K-z)^\circ|$ over all interior centers $z$.
-- L4DEL: step: The hypotheses require positive dimension, compactness, convexity, and nonempty interior. No central symmetry, smooth boundary, or strict convexity is assumed.
-- L4DEL: step: The conclusion is a conjunction: the sharp lower bound holds, and equality is equivalent to the existence of $n+1$ affinely independent vertices whose convex hull is exactly $K$.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the nonsymmetric theorem. The stronger constant $4^n/n!$ for centrally symmetric bodies is a different statement, already exposed separately on the symmetric-Mahler page. The manuscript's functional inequality is outside this comparator target. The body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.Geometry.Convex.GeneralMahler.Theorem`.
-- L4DEL: term: Santaló point = the unique interior center that minimizes the volume of the translated polar body
-- L4DEL: term: volume product = the volume of a convex body multiplied by the volume of its polar at an optimizing interior center
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI general-Mahler manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/The-Mahler-Conjecture-for-General-Convex-Bodies-September-22-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 087 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/087.md
-- L4DEL: source: Kurt Mahler, original polygon paper bibliographic record = https://mathconjectures.com/references/Mahler1939
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI general-Mahler manuscript (abstract) = with Santaló point s(K), |K| |(K − s(K))◦ | ≥ (n + 1)n+1 /(n!)2 , with equality exactly for simplices
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI general-Mahler manuscript (abstract) = The symmetric Mahler conjecture asks for the different, stronger constant 4n /n! on the restricted class of centrally symmetric bodies

@file ComparatorChallenges/ThompsonNonamenability.lean
@decl OAI.ThompsonNonamenability.thompson_F_nonamenable_composition
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Thompson's group F has no invariant mean
-- L4DEL: intuition: Thompson's group $F$ is modeled by increasing, dyadic piecewise-linear homeomorphisms of the unit interval. The theorem supplies the composition group law and rules out every positive normalized left-invariant mean on its bounded real-valued functions; that is the asserted nonamenability.
-- L4DEL: step: The existentially supplied `Group F` must multiply homeomorphisms by composition: `(h * g)(x) = h(g(x))` for every pair of elements and every point of the unit interval.
-- L4DEL: step: `InvariantMean F` packages a real-linear functional on bounded functions `F → ℝ`, positivity on nonnegative functions, normalization at the constant one function, and invariance under every left translation.
-- L4DEL: step: The second conjunct is `¬ Nonempty (InvariantMean F)`. The Lean statement gives no explicit Følner-boundary constant and fixes no prescribed generating set.
-- L4DEL: step: Cannon, Floyd, and Parry explained why either answer would place this finitely presented group in an exceptional structural class. The linked OpenAI manuscript claims Geoghegan's 1979 nonamenability conjecture. This comparator body is `sorry`, paired with `OAI.GroupTheory.Thompson.Main`.
-- L4DEL: term: amenable group = a group admitting a positive normalized mean on bounded real functions that is unchanged by left translation
-- L4DEL: term: dyadic piecewise-linear homeomorphism = an increasing interval homeomorphism with finitely many dyadic breakpoints and slopes that are integer powers of two
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Thompson-F manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Thompsons-group-F-is-nonamenable-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 248 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/248.md
-- L4DEL: source: Cannon, Floyd, and Parry (1996), p. 228 = https://www.e-periodica.ch/cntmng?pid=ens-001%3A1996%3A42%3A%3A85
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Thompson-F manuscript (abstract) = This confirms Geoghegan’s conjecture and resolves the amenability problem for F
-- L4DEL: quote: Cannon, Floyd, and Parry (1996), p. 228 = if F is not amenable, then F is a finitely presented group in NF ∖ AG

@file ComparatorChallenges/AlgorithmicThinTrees.lean
@decl OAI.StrongThinTree.MultiGraph
-- L4DEL: title: A finite loopless multigraph with individually indexed edge copies
-- L4DEL: intuition: `MultiGraph n m` has vertices `Fin n` and edge copies `Fin m`. Each edge copy has a left and right endpoint, and `loopless` rules out an edge whose two endpoints coincide.
-- L4DEL: step: Parallel edges are allowed because two different members of `Fin m` may have the same endpoint pair.
-- L4DEL: step: The edge index is therefore an identity for a copy, not merely a pair of vertices. That distinction matters when cuts, trees, and binary multiplicities count parallel copies.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = every parallel copy counts separately

@decl OAI.StrongThinTree.MultiGraph.cut
-- L4DEL: title: The selected edges crossing a vertex cut
-- L4DEL: intuition: `G.cut T S` keeps exactly those edge copies in `T` with one endpoint in the vertex set `S` and the other endpoint outside it.
-- L4DEL: step: The two disjuncts cover both endpoint orientations, so the definition describes an undirected cut even though every edge stores `left` and `right` endpoints.
-- L4DEL: step: Supplying `T = Finset.univ` gives the whole graph cut. Supplying a proposed tree edge set gives the part of that cut used by the tree.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = every parallel copy counts separately

@decl OAI.StrongThinTree.MultiGraph.Connected
-- L4DEL: title: Connectivity of a chosen edge set
-- L4DEL: intuition: `G.Connected T` says every nonempty proper set of vertices has at least one edge of `T` crossing to its complement.
-- L4DEL: step: This cut condition is the finite-graph characterization of connectedness; it is stated directly so it also applies to a selected subset of multigraph edge copies.
-- L4DEL: step: Empty and full vertex sets are excluded because their cuts are necessarily empty and say nothing about connectivity.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = every parallel copy counts separately

@decl OAI.StrongThinTree.MultiGraph.SpanningTree
-- L4DEL: title: A minimally connected spanning edge set
-- L4DEL: intuition: `G.SpanningTree T` means `T` connects all vertices and deleting any one edge of `T` destroys that connectivity.
-- L4DEL: step: The vertex set is already all of `Fin n`; “spanning” is expressed by testing cuts of the entire vertex type rather than by storing a separate vertex subset.
-- L4DEL: step: Minimal connectedness supplies the acyclicity normally used in the definition of a finite spanning tree.
-- L4DEL: source: Luis Goddyn, Thin Trees conjecture page (archived) = https://web.archive.org/web/20211025204852/https://www.sfu.ca/~goddyn/Problems/problems.html
-- L4DEL: quote: Luis Goddyn, Thin Trees conjecture page (archived) = The problem is to make the same tree thin on every cocircuit B.

@decl OAI.StrongThinTree.MultiGraph.EdgeConnected
-- L4DEL: title: At least $k$ edge copies cross every nontrivial cut
-- L4DEL: intuition: `G.EdgeConnected k` says each nonempty proper vertex set has a whole-graph cut containing at least `k` edge copies.
-- L4DEL: step: The call `G.cut Finset.univ S` counts every indexed edge copy of `G`, including parallel copies.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the hypothesis that provides the denominator scale in the desired $C/k$ thinness bound.
-- L4DEL: source: Nima Anari and Shayan Oveis Gharan, Effective-Resistance-Reducing Flows, Spectrally Thin Trees, and Asymmetric TSP = https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.4613v4
-- L4DEL: quote: Nima Anari and Shayan Oveis Gharan, Effective-Resistance-Reducing Flows, Spectrally Thin Trees, and Asymmetric TSP = any k-edge-connected unweighted graph has a polyloglog(n)/k-thin spanning tree.

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.frame
-- L4DEL: title: A self-delimiting Boolean-list frame
-- L4DEL: intuition: `frame` encodes each payload bit as `false` followed by that bit, then terminates the framed list with `true`.
-- L4DEL: step: The empty payload becomes `[true]`; a nonempty payload begins with `false`, so a decoder can distinguish continuation from termination without an external length.
-- L4DEL: step: This is a serialization helper for the formal stack-machine input, not a mathematical ingredient of the thin-tree theorem.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.encodeNat
-- L4DEL: title: A framed bit encoding of a natural number
-- L4DEL: intuition: `encodeNat n` applies the file's self-delimiting `frame` to Mathlib's computability-oriented Boolean encoding of `n`.
-- L4DEL: step: Framing lets multiple natural-number encodings be concatenated while preserving their boundaries.
-- L4DEL: step: The theorem measures runtime against the length of the resulting encoded input, so this choice participates in the formal complexity statement.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.StackAction
-- L4DEL: title: The three permitted updates to one Boolean stack
-- L4DEL: intuition: A `StackAction` either leaves a stack alone, removes its head, or pushes one specified Boolean bit.
-- L4DEL: step: An instruction chooses one such action independently for each stack.
-- L4DEL: step: This deliberately small instruction language makes the existential algorithm a concrete finite machine rather than an unspecified Lean function.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.StackAction.apply
-- L4DEL: title: Execute one action on one Boolean stack
-- L4DEL: intuition: `stay` returns the stack unchanged, `pop` takes its tail, and `push b` prepends `b`.
-- L4DEL: step: Lean's `List.tail` makes popping an empty stack return the empty stack, so this transition operation is total.
-- L4DEL: step: `Machine.step` applies these actions simultaneously to all indexed stacks.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.Instruction
-- L4DEL: title: One transition of a finite multi-stack machine
-- L4DEL: intuition: An `Instruction k q` contains an optional next state among `q` states and one stack action for each of `k` stacks.
-- L4DEL: step: A present next state continues computation; `none` represents a halted configuration.
-- L4DEL: step: Because both the state and stack indices are finite, a `Machine` can store a finite transition table for these instructions.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.Instruction.halt
-- L4DEL: title: The default halting instruction
-- L4DEL: intuition: `Instruction.halt k q` sets the next state to `none` and leaves every stack unchanged.
-- L4DEL: step: `Machine.step` uses this instruction when a computed transition-table index is absent.
-- L4DEL: step: Thus an incomplete finite table has a defined, conservative behavior rather than an out-of-bounds transition.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.Machine
-- L4DEL: title: A finite Boolean-stack machine with designated input and output stacks
-- L4DEL: intuition: `Machine` records finite numbers of stacks and states, an initial state, designated input and output stacks, and a finite instruction table.
-- L4DEL: step: The fields use `Fin` indices, so constructing a machine also supplies proofs that its initial state and designated stacks exist within the declared sizes.
-- L4DEL: step: This is the formalization's computational model for “deterministic algorithm”; the manuscript does not claim that its mathematical algorithm is literally presented as this Lean table.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.Config
-- L4DEL: title: A machine state together with all current stacks
-- L4DEL: intuition: `Config k q` stores either a current state among `q` states or `none` for halting, plus one Boolean list for each of `k` stacks.
-- L4DEL: step: A configuration is the complete instantaneous state consumed and produced by `Machine.step`.
-- L4DEL: step: Halting is represented inside the configuration, allowing the final proposition to assert `result.state = none` after bounded fuel.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.topCode
-- L4DEL: title: Encode a stack's visible head as one of three cases
-- L4DEL: intuition: `topCode` returns `0` for an empty stack, `1` for a leading `false`, and `2` for a leading `true`.
-- L4DEL: step: Only the top cell is visible to a single machine transition.
-- L4DEL: step: The three-valued code is one base-$3$ digit used by `headsCode` to select a table instruction.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.headsCode
-- L4DEL: title: Combine all visible stack heads into one base-$3$ number
-- L4DEL: intuition: `headsCode k s` recursively packs the empty/false/true status of each of the `k` stack heads into a natural number.
-- L4DEL: step: Stack zero is the least significant base-$3$ digit; subsequent stacks contribute after multiplication by `3`.
-- L4DEL: step: The result ranges over the observation patterns used to index the machine's finite transition table.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.Machine.step
-- L4DEL: title: Perform one deterministic machine transition
-- L4DEL: intuition: A halted configuration stays fixed. Otherwise the current state and all visible stack heads select one instruction, whose next state and stack actions produce the next configuration.
-- L4DEL: step: The table index is `3 ^ stackCount * state + headsCode`, separating each state's block of stack-head patterns.
-- L4DEL: step: `getD` chooses the halting instruction when that index lies beyond the finite table, and each selected stack action is applied to its corresponding stack.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.Machine.init
-- L4DEL: title: Initialize the machine from an encoded input
-- L4DEL: intuition: `M.init input` starts in `M.initial`, places the entire Boolean input on `M.inputStack`, and makes every other stack empty.
-- L4DEL: step: The output stack is not granted any special initial contents; it is merely the stack read after the bounded run.
-- L4DEL: step: If input and output designations coincide, the structure permits that explicitly rather than assuming distinct stacks.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.CurrentKS.Machine.run
-- L4DEL: title: Run a machine for exactly the supplied fuel
-- L4DEL: intuition: `M.run input fuel` initializes `M` and iterates its one-step transition exactly `fuel` times.
-- L4DEL: step: Because halted configurations are fixed points of `step`, extra fuel after halting does not change the result.
-- L4DEL: step: The final proposition supplies polynomial fuel and then explicitly requires the resulting state to be `none`; fuel alone does not assert termination.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.ExplicitInput
-- L4DEL: title: A thin-tree instance with every edge copy listed explicitly
-- L4DEL: intuition: `ExplicitInput` packages the number of vertices `n`, number of edge copies `m`, connectivity parameter `k`, and an actual `MultiGraph n m`.
-- L4DEL: step: Parallel copies occupy separate positions of `Fin m`, so the input size grows with the total expanded multiplicity.
-- L4DEL: step: This representation is one branch of the common `Input` type handled by the claimed machine.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = every parallel copy counts separately

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.BinaryInput
-- L4DEL: title: A multiplicity-compressed multigraph input
-- L4DEL: intuition: `BinaryInput` lists `p` distinct ordered endpoint pairs and assigns a natural-number multiplicity to each pair instead of enumerating every parallel edge copy.
-- L4DEL: step: `ordered` proves the endpoints differ and chooses a canonical orientation `left < right`; `distinct` prevents the same unordered pair from appearing twice.
-- L4DEL: step: Large multiplicities remain compact when `BinaryInput.encode` serializes those natural numbers in binary-oriented form.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.BinaryInput.Edge
-- L4DEL: title: One concrete edge copy in a compressed binary input
-- L4DEL: intuition: `x.Edge` is a dependent pair: an endpoint-pair index `i`, followed by a copy index below `x.multiplicity i`.
-- L4DEL: step: A pair with multiplicity zero contributes no inhabitants because `Fin 0` is empty.
-- L4DEL: step: This expands multiplicities mathematically while the serialized input can remain compressed.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = every parallel copy counts separately

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.BinaryInput.edgeEquiv
-- L4DEL: title: Number all expanded binary-input edge copies
-- L4DEL: intuition: `edgeEquiv` chooses an equivalence between the finite type `x.Edge` and consecutive indices `Fin (Fintype.card x.Edge)`.
-- L4DEL: step: It is `noncomputable` because Lean selects a finite equivalence abstractly rather than defining a particular executable ordering.
-- L4DEL: step: Later definitions use this equivalence consistently to build a `MultiGraph` and translate decoded output edges back to its indices.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = every parallel copy counts separately

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.BinaryInput.graph
-- L4DEL: title: Expand compressed multiplicities into a loopless multigraph
-- L4DEL: intuition: `x.graph` turns every inhabitant of `x.Edge` into its own indexed edge copy and assigns it the endpoints of its pair index.
-- L4DEL: step: The inverse of `edgeEquiv` recovers the dependent pair from a consecutive edge index.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof `left < right` supplied by `BinaryInput.ordered` establishes that every expanded copy is loopless.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = every parallel copy counts separately

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.Input
-- L4DEL: title: Either an explicit or multiplicity-compressed graph instance
-- L4DEL: intuition: `Input` is a tagged union whose `explicit` branch contains every edge copy and whose `binary` branch contains endpoint pairs with multiplicities.
-- L4DEL: step: Subsequent definitions pattern-match on this tag so validation, size, decoding, and correctness have one common interface.
-- L4DEL: step: The final algorithmic proposition requires one fixed machine to handle both representations.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.ExplicitInput.encode
-- L4DEL: title: Serialize an explicitly listed multigraph
-- L4DEL: intuition: The encoding concatenates framed natural numbers for `n`, `m`, and `k`, then for every edge index concatenates its two endpoint indices.
-- L4DEL: step: `List.ofFn` traverses the finite edge-index type, and `flatten` joins the per-edge encodings into one Boolean list.
-- L4DEL: step: Since every parallel copy appears separately, the encoded length reflects the expanded edge count.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = every parallel copy counts separately

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.BinaryInput.encode
-- L4DEL: title: Serialize a multiplicity-compressed multigraph
-- L4DEL: intuition: The encoding writes `n`, the number `p` of endpoint pairs, and `k`, followed by each pair's endpoints and multiplicity.
-- L4DEL: step: It does not emit one record per edge copy; a multiplicity is emitted once as a framed natural number.
-- L4DEL: step: The theorem's runtime bound uses this compressed Boolean-list length, so it is stronger than polynomial time in an expanded graph when multiplicities are huge.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.Input.encode
-- L4DEL: title: Tag and serialize either input representation
-- L4DEL: intuition: `Input.encode` prefixes `false` for an explicit input and `true` for a binary-multiplicity input, then appends that branch's encoding.
-- L4DEL: step: The first bit makes the two formats distinguishable to the one machine required by `AlgorithmicStrongThinTrees`.
-- L4DEL: step: The branch encoders then provide self-delimiting framed naturals for the graph data.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.Input.length
-- L4DEL: title: The bit length used by the polynomial runtime bound
-- L4DEL: intuition: `x.length` is exactly the length of `x.encode`, including the representation tag and all framing bits.
-- L4DEL: step: `AlgorithmicStrongThinTrees` supplies fuel polynomial in `x.length + 1`.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an encoding-dependent complexity measure; it is not the number of vertices, endpoint pairs, or expanded edge copies alone.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.Input.n
-- L4DEL: title: Read the vertex count from either input branch
-- L4DEL: intuition: `Input.n` returns the stored number of vertices whether the graph was represented explicitly or by binary multiplicities.
-- L4DEL: step: `GoodOutput` uses this common projection to impose the special empty output for a one-vertex graph.
-- L4DEL: step: No decoding or graph expansion is needed to obtain this value.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = in time polynomial in the total binary input length

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.Valid
-- L4DEL: title: The admissible $k$-edge-connected inputs
-- L4DEL: intuition: An input is `Valid` when it has at least one vertex, has positive `k`, and its explicit or expanded multigraph is $k$-edge-connected.
-- L4DEL: step: The two branches impose the same mathematical conditions after the binary branch expands its multiplicities into edge copies.
-- L4DEL: step: The final proposition promises behavior only under this predicate; malformed or insufficiently connected encodings are outside its guarantee.
-- L4DEL: source: Nima Anari and Shayan Oveis Gharan, Effective-Resistance-Reducing Flows, Spectrally Thin Trees, and Asymmetric TSP = https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.4613v4
-- L4DEL: quote: Nima Anari and Shayan Oveis Gharan, Effective-Resistance-Reducing Flows, Spectrally Thin Trees, and Asymmetric TSP = any k-edge-connected unweighted graph has a polyloglog(n)/k-thin spanning tree.

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.ThinTree
-- L4DEL: title: A spanning tree uniformly thin across every cut
-- L4DEL: intuition: `ThinTree C k G T` requires `T` to be a spanning tree and to use at most a $C/k$ fraction of the whole graph's edges across every nontrivial vertex cut.
-- L4DEL: step: Both sides count indexed edge copies, so parallel multiplicity contributes to the tree cut and the graph cut in the same model.
-- L4DEL: step: The same `T` must satisfy the inequality for all cuts; it is not allowed to choose a different tree for each cut.
-- L4DEL: source: Luis Goddyn, Thin Trees conjecture page (archived) = https://web.archive.org/web/20211025204852/https://www.sfu.ca/~goddyn/Problems/problems.html
-- L4DEL: quote: Luis Goddyn, Thin Trees conjecture page (archived) = The problem is to make the same tree thin on every cocircuit B.
-- L4DEL: source: Nima Anari and Shayan Oveis Gharan, Effective-Resistance-Reducing Flows, Spectrally Thin Trees, and Asymmetric TSP = https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.4613v4
-- L4DEL: quote: Nima Anari and Shayan Oveis Gharan, Effective-Resistance-Reducing Flows, Spectrally Thin Trees, and Asymmetric TSP = any k-edge-connected unweighted graph has a polyloglog(n)/k-thin spanning tree.

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.ExplicitInput.encodeEdges
-- L4DEL: title: Serialize a list of explicit edge indices
-- L4DEL: intuition: `encodeEdges` concatenates the framed natural-number value of every explicit edge index in the proposed output list.
-- L4DEL: step: The output carries only edge identities; the input graph supplies their endpoints.
-- L4DEL: step: `TreeOutput` separately requires the decoded list to contain no duplicate edge indices.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = every parallel copy counts separately

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.BinaryInput.encodeEdges
-- L4DEL: title: Serialize selected copies from a compressed multigraph
-- L4DEL: intuition: Each output edge is encoded by its endpoint-pair index and its copy index within that pair's multiplicity.
-- L4DEL: step: This pair of naturals identifies one inhabitant of the dependent edge-copy type `x.Edge`.
-- L4DEL: step: The encoding therefore distinguishes parallel copies without expanding the input representation itself.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = every parallel copy counts separately

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.BinaryInput.selected
-- L4DEL: title: Convert decoded binary-input edge copies to multigraph indices
-- L4DEL: intuition: `selected` removes repetitions from a decoded edge list and maps each dependent edge copy through `edgeEquiv` into the `Fin` indices used by `x.graph`.
-- L4DEL: step: The result is a `Finset`, which is the representation expected by `SpanningTree`, `cut`, and `ThinTree`.
-- L4DEL: step: This definition is `noncomputable` for the same reason as `edgeEquiv`; it specifies output correctness rather than implementing the stack machine's decoder.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = every parallel copy counts separately

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.TreeOutput
-- L4DEL: title: The output bits encode a duplicate-free thin spanning tree
-- L4DEL: intuition: `TreeOutput C x bits` means there exists a duplicate-free list of valid edge copies whose branch-specific serialization is exactly `bits` and whose selected edge set is `ThinTree C`.
-- L4DEL: step: In the explicit branch, edge indices already refer directly to `x.graph`; in the binary branch, dependent edge copies are translated by `x.selected`.
-- L4DEL: step: The existential decoded list is the certificate connecting raw output bits to the mathematical thin-tree property.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = meeting every cut in at most a C/k fraction

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.GoodOutput
-- L4DEL: title: A valid tree output with a canonical one-vertex case
-- L4DEL: intuition: `GoodOutput C x bits` requires a `TreeOutput` and additionally requires `bits = []` whenever the input has exactly one vertex.
-- L4DEL: step: A one-vertex graph's spanning tree has no edges, and the extra clause fixes its serialization to the empty Boolean list.
-- L4DEL: step: For larger inputs, all output requirements come from `TreeOutput`.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = meeting every cut in at most a C/k fraction

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.AlgorithmicStrongThinTrees
-- L4DEL: title: The complete deterministic polynomial-time strong thin-tree proposition
-- L4DEL: intuition: There exist one positive real constant `C`, one fixed finite stack machine `M`, and natural numbers `a` and `degree` with `a > 0` such that every valid input is solved within `a * (length + 1) ^ degree` steps.
-- L4DEL: step: The result after that exact fuel must be halted, and the designated output stack must satisfy `GoodOutput C` for the original input.
-- L4DEL: step: Quantifying over `C`, `M`, `a`, and `degree` before `x` makes them uniform across all graph sizes, connectivities, and both encodings.
-- L4DEL: step: This proposition specifies an algorithm and its certificate format. It does not itself construct the machine; the following theorem is intended to prove this proposition but currently has body `by sorry`.
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = meeting every cut in at most a C/k fraction
-- L4DEL: source: Nima Anari and Shayan Oveis Gharan, Effective-Resistance-Reducing Flows, Spectrally Thin Trees, and Asymmetric TSP = https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.4613v4
-- L4DEL: quote: Nima Anari and Shayan Oveis Gharan, Effective-Resistance-Reducing Flows, Spectrally Thin Trees, and Asymmetric TSP = any k-edge-connected unweighted graph has a polyloglog(n)/k-thin spanning tree.

@decl OAI.AlgorithmicThinTrees.algorithmic_strong_thin_trees
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: A deterministic polynomial-time strong thin-tree construction
-- L4DEL: intuition: There is one universal constant $C>0$ and one deterministic finite stack machine that, from either of the statement's two encodings of any finite loopless $k$-edge-connected multigraph, halts in polynomially many steps and outputs a spanning tree whose share of every nontrivial cut is at most $C/k$ times the graph's share of that cut.
-- L4DEL: step: `Input` has two cases. `explicit` lists every edge separately; `binary` lists distinct endpoint pairs and binary-encoded parallel-edge multiplicities. `Valid` requires at least one vertex, positive $k$, and at least $k$ graph edges across every nontrivial cut.
-- L4DEL: step: `AlgorithmicStrongThinTrees` supplies a positive real constant `C`, a concrete `CurrentKS.Machine`, a positive natural coefficient `a`, and a natural exponent `degree` that may be zero. On every valid encoding the machine must halt within `a * (x.length + 1) ^ degree` steps.
-- L4DEL: step: The output decodes to a duplicate-free edge list forming a spanning tree `T`, and every nonempty proper vertex set $S$ satisfies $|\delta_T(S)|\le (C/k)|\delta_G(S)|$. For the one-vertex case the bit output is required to be empty.
-- L4DEL: step: Nima Anari's Simons Institute lecture states the strong form associated with Luis Goddyn as an existence conjecture. The pinned manuscript's additional claim is constructive: one deterministic polynomial-time algorithm works even when parallel multiplicities are compressed in binary.
-- L4DEL: step: Trust boundary: this comparator declaration ends in `by sorry`. The pinned comparator configuration pairs it with `OAI.Combinatorics.ThinTrees.AlgorithmicMain`; the site's Lean trust result, not this prose or the manuscript, determines whether that separate solution module checks.
-- L4DEL: term: strong thin tree = a spanning tree that crosses every cut using at most an $O(1/k)$ fraction of the original cut in a $k$-edge-connected graph
-- L4DEL: term: edge connectivity = the minimum number of graph edges crossing any nonempty proper vertex cut
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-polynomial-time-construction-of-strong-thin-trees-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 174 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/174.md
-- L4DEL: source: Nima Anari, Thin Trees and Interlacing Families lecture slides (Simons Institute) = https://simons.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/docs/11322/presentation.pdf
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI algorithmic strong-thin-tree manuscript = meeting every cut in at most a C/k fraction
-- L4DEL: quote: Nima Anari, Thin Trees and Interlacing Families lecture slides (Simons Institute) = Every k-edge connected graph has O(1/k)-thin spanning tree.

@file ComparatorChallenges/BarnetteHamiltonian.lean
@decl OAI.Barnette.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Barnette's Hamiltonian-cycle conjecture
-- L4DEL: intuition: Every finite simple planar graph that is cubic, bipartite, and remains connected after deletion of any two vertices has one cycle visiting every vertex exactly once.
-- L4DEL: step: The statement quantifies over every finite vertex type and simple graph. Its four hypotheses are degree-three regularity, bipartiteness, a crossing-free topological plane embedding, and `ThreeVertexConnected`.
-- L4DEL: step: `ThreeVertexConnected` also requires at least four vertices. `HasHamiltonianCycle` asks for a closed walk satisfying Mathlib's `IsHamiltonianCycle`; it is a single spanning cycle, not a disconnected spanning 2-factor.
-- L4DEL: step: David Barnette posed the conjecture in 1969 in the language of cubic bipartite polyhedral graphs. By Steinitz's theorem, `polyhedral` corresponds here to planar and 3-vertex-connected.
-- L4DEL: step: The pinned manuscript's page-one strategy passes to the spherical dual triangulation and constructs an acyclic dual edge set whose complement becomes one connected spanning cycle; the Lean statement exposes only the theorem, not that prose proof outline.
-- L4DEL: step: Trust boundary: this comparator declaration ends in `by sorry`. The pinned comparator configuration pairs it with `OAI.Combinatorics.Hamiltonian.Main`; the site's Lean trust result, not this annotation, determines whether that separate solution module checks.
-- L4DEL: term: cubic graph = a graph in which every vertex has degree three
-- L4DEL: term: Hamiltonian cycle = one cycle that visits every vertex exactly once before returning to its start
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Barnette manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Paired-states-and-Hamiltonian-cycles-in-cubic-bipartite-planar-graphs-September-24-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 180 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/180.md
-- L4DEL: source: Lean Arts, Meike Hopman, and Veerle Timmermans, Barnette's Conjecture (2013) = https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.5504
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Barnette manuscript (abstract) = We prove Barnette’s conjecture: every finite simple cubic bipartite planar 3-vertex-connected graph has a Hamiltonian cycle.
-- L4DEL: quote: Lean Arts, Meike Hopman, and Veerle Timmermans, Barnette's Conjecture (2013) = Every cubic, bipartite, polyhedral graph contains a Hamiltonian cycle.

@file ComparatorChallenges/ArtinCAT0.lean
@decl OAI.ArtinCAT0.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: An explicit Artin group with no geometric $\mathrm{CAT}(0)$ action
-- L4DEL: intuition: An explicitly presented Artin group on 116 generators cannot act geometrically on any nonempty proper $\mathrm{CAT}(0)$ metric space, in any dimension.
-- L4DEL: step: `explicitMatrix` is proved symmetric, has diagonal entries one, and has every off-diagonal label in $\{2,3,\infty\}$. Finite labels impose alternating Artin relations; the label $\infty$ imposes no relation.
-- L4DEL: step: `GeometricAction` combines four requirements: the metric space satisfies the stated $\mathrm{CAT}(0)$ triangle inequality, every group element acts isometrically, the action is proper, and one compact set has translates covering the space.
-- L4DEL: step: The final conjunct quantifies over every metric space, proper-space structure, nonempty witness, and action of this exact Artin group. It therefore refutes the group-level CAT(0) conjecture, not merely one proposed metric on one standard complex.
-- L4DEL: step: Ruth Charney's survey recorded the proper-cocompact-isometric-action question as open for spherical and non-spherical Artin groups. The Lean target gives one explicit counterexample rather than classifying all Artin groups.
-- L4DEL: step: Trust boundary: this comparator declaration ends in `by sorry`. The pinned comparator configuration pairs it with `OAI.GroupTheory.ArtinCAT0.Main`; the site's Lean trust result, not this annotation, determines whether that separate solution module checks.
-- L4DEL: term: Artin group = a group presented by generators whose pairwise relations equate alternating words of lengths prescribed by a Coxeter matrix
-- L4DEL: term: geometric CAT(0) action = a proper and cocompact action by isometries on a nonempty proper CAT(0) metric space
-- L4DEL: acronym: CAT = Cartan-Alexandrov-Toponogov
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI Artin-CAT(0) manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/An-Artin-group-with-no-geometric-CAT-0-action-September-23-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 254 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/254.md
-- L4DEL: source: Ruth Charney, An introduction to right-angled Artin groups (2007) = https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0610668
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI Artin-CAT(0) manuscript (abstract) = We construct an Artin group on 116 generators that admits no proper, cocompact isometric action on a nonempty proper CAT(0) space.
-- L4DEL: quote: Ruth Charney, An introduction to right-angled Artin groups (2007) = One question which is still open for both spherical and non-spherical Artin groups is whether they are CAT(0) groups

@file ComparatorChallenges/AmplitudeDamping.lean
@decl OAI.GAD.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Exact unassisted classical capacity of generalized amplitude damping
-- L4DEL: intuition: For every damping strength and thermal occupation in $[0,1]$, the unrestricted $n$-use Holevo information is exactly $n$ times its one-use value, an explicit one-variable maximum attains that value, and the operational unassisted classical capacity equals it.
-- L4DEL: step: `holevo γ ν n` optimizes over all finite ensembles of $n$-qubit density matrices; inputs need not be pure, product, or equiprobable. The first conjunct proves additivity across every positive block length.
-- L4DEL: step: The second conjunct supplies a maximizer $p\in[0,1]$ for the scalar `objective γ ν p` and identifies every block optimum with $n/\log 2$ times that objective.
-- L4DEL: step: The third conjunct identifies the operational capacity, defined through codes with collective output measurements and vanishing average error, with the one-use Holevo value. The fourth verifies that every maximizing equiprobable phase-pair product ensemble attains every block optimum.
-- L4DEL: step: Scope boundary: unlike the stronger pinned manuscript abstract, this comparator theorem does not state additivity with an arbitrary different partner channel or minimum-output-entropy additivity. It covers repeated uses of the same generalized amplitude-damping channel.
-- L4DEL: step: Khatri, Sharma, and Wilde previously established parameter ranges and upper bounds for generalized amplitude-damping capacities. The cited OpenAI result claims an exact unrestricted classical-capacity formula beyond those bounds.
-- L4DEL: step: Trust boundary: this comparator declaration ends in `by sorry`. The pinned comparator configuration pairs it with `OAI.InformationTheory.AmplitudeDamping.Main`; the site's Lean trust result, not this annotation, determines whether that separate solution module checks.
-- L4DEL: term: generalized amplitude-damping channel = a qubit noise channel modeling energy relaxation in contact with a thermal environment
-- L4DEL: term: Holevo information = the entropy of an ensemble's average output minus the ensemble-average output entropy
-- L4DEL: acronym: GAD = generalized amplitude damping
-- L4DEL: source: OpenAI generalized-amplitude-damping manuscript (abstract) = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Classical-capacity-and-entropy-inequalities-for-generalized-amplitude-damping-September-24-2026/paper.pdf
-- L4DEL: source: Pinned OpenAI formalization note 276 = https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/lean/docs/276.md
-- L4DEL: source: Sumeet Khatri, Kunal Sharma, and Mark M. Wilde, Information-theoretic aspects of GAD (2020) = https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.07747v2
-- L4DEL: quote: OpenAI generalized-amplitude-damping manuscript (abstract) = It equals the one-shot Holevo capacity and is given by a one-variable maximum.
-- L4DEL: quote: Sumeet Khatri, Kunal Sharma, and Mark M. Wilde, Information-theoretic aspects of GAD (2020) = We then establish several upper bounds on its classical, quantum, and private capacities.

