@file ComparatorChallenges/CharacterCriterion.lean
@decl OAI.KirchbergRordam.character_criterion
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: The Kirchberg–Rørdam character criterion and infinite tensor-power Jiang–Su stability
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Kirchberg–Rørdam criterion relates Jiang–Su absorption to characters of the central-sequence algebra. The formalized result proves that, for every nonzero unital separable complex $C^*$-algebra $A$ and every free ultrafilter on $\mathbb N$, the norm central-sequence algebra has no nonzero character exactly when $A\cong A\otimes_{\min}\mathcal Z$. No nuclearity, simplicity, trace, or comparison hypothesis is imposed.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Analysis.CharacterCriterion.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/CharacterVarietiesAllSeamsSupport.lean
@decl OAI.IntegralCharacterVarieties.SurfacePresentation.Diagram.producedMarkedValues_allSeams
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Potential integral density on curve character varieties
-- L4DEL: intuition: The integral-density question asks whether integral points become Zariski dense in every component of a curve character variety after one finite extension of the number field. The linked formalization proves a supporting compatibility result for its marked-surface construction. Given a proper diagram with finitely many vertices, a chosen seam with maximal adjacent facet rank and a positive-genus parent, puncture data, an existing solution over a field, and a chosen boundary occurrence, the produced marked values satisfy every seam equation. This is conditional compatibility of the produced solution. Potential integral density, including the prescribed-boundary and all-component conclusions, remains outside the selected statement.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.AlgebraicGeometry.CharacterVarieties.Seams.ProducedSolution as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/CHObstruction.lean
@decl OAI.CHObstruction.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Shelah's eventual categoricity and the prescribed-threshold obstruction
-- L4DEL: intuition: Under the continuum hypothesis, the formalized result refutes the proposed transfer of categoricity down to the Hanf threshold. It gives an abstract elementary class in a countable relational language, with Löwenheim–Skolem number $\aleph_0$, that has two nonisomorphic models at $\beth_{\omega_2}=h(\aleph_0)$ but is categorical in every cardinal at least $\beth_{(2^{\aleph_1})^+}$. No amalgamation, joint embedding, tameness, or absence-of-maximal-models assumption is imposed. The later canonical-point obstruction and the separate eventual-categoricity theorem are not included.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.ModelTheory.Categoricity.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

