@decl OAI.CubicTorus.unit_cubic_isoperimetric
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: The isoperimetric profile of the cubic three-torus
-- L4DEL: intuition: The formalization determines the isoperimetric profile and all minimizers in the unit cubic flat three-torus. At every volume $0<V<1$, a minimizer exists, and the minimizers, up to null-set changes, are exactly balls, circular tubes about shortest closed geodesics, coordinate slabs, and their complements in the appropriate volume ranges. The classification includes all equality cases at the transition volumes $4\pi/81$ and $1/\pi$, and every minimizer has the stated candidate-profile perimeter.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Geometry.CubicTorus.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/CycleCliqueRamsey.lean
@decl OAI.CycleClique.thm_main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Cycle–clique Ramsey numbers
-- L4DEL: intuition: The cycle–clique Ramsey conjecture predicts the exact number of vertices forcing either a cycle $C_m$ or a clique $K_n$ in complementary colors. The formalization proves $R(C_m,K_n)=(m-1)(n-1)+1$ for all integers $m\ge n\ge3$ except $(m,n)=(3,3)$, where it proves $R(C_3,K_3)=6$. This is the complete parameter range of the paper's main theorem.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Combinatorics.Ramsey.CycleClique.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/CycleDecomposition.lean
@decl OAI.ErdosGallai.erdos_gallai
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: The Erdős–Gallai cycle-decomposition conjecture
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Erdős–Gallai cycle-decomposition conjecture asks for a linear bound on the number of cycles and single edges needed to partition a graph's edges. The formalized result gives one absolute constant $C>0$ such that every finite simple graph on $n$ vertices has an edge-disjoint decomposition into at most $Cn$ cycles or singleton edges. Edgeless and small graphs are included. The optimal value of $C$ is not determined.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Combinatorics.CycleDecomposition.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

