@file ComparatorChallenges/DegreeRigidity.lean
@decl OAI.TuringRigidity.ManuscriptMain.rigidity
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Rigidity of the Turing degrees
-- L4DEL: intuition: The rigidity problem asks whether the ordering of Turing degrees by relative computability has any nontrivial automorphism. The formalized result gives a negative answer: every order automorphism of the full Turing degrees of subsets of $\mathbb N$ fixes every degree, with no definability or genericity assumption. The additional representation corollaries are not included.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Computability.DegreeRigidity.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/DeligneDrinfeld.lean
@decl OAI.DeligneDrinfeld.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: The Deligne–Drinfeld conjecture
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Deligne–Drinfeld conjecture predicts that the rational Grothendieck–Teichmüller Lie algebra is freely generated by one element in each odd weight $3,5,7,\ldots$. The formalization establishes this for the rational solution space of antisymmetry, the three-term equation, and the four-strand pentagon with the Ihara bracket. It also gives the corresponding continuous isomorphism after completion by weight. The regularized-transport proposition and graph-complex consequences are not included.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Algebra.Drinfeld.Completion as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/DepthFive.lean
@decl OAI.Problem335.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Homogeneous depth-five lower bounds for iterated matrix multiplication
-- L4DEL: intuition: Let $\mathrm{IMM}_{n,n}$ be the $(1,1)$ entry of the product of $n$ independent $n\times n$ variable matrices. The formalization proves that, over every characteristic-zero field, syntactically homogeneous depth-five $\Sigma\Pi\Sigma\Pi\Sigma$ circuits computing this polynomial require at least $n^{\sqrt n/400}$ gates for all sufficiently large $n$, with a threshold independent of the field. Arbitrary bottom support, finite fan-in and fan-out, and sharing are allowed. It also gives, over every field and for $n\ge2$, circuits with at most $n^{\sqrt n+4}$ gates. These are the lower and upper bounds selected from the paper.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Algebra.DepthFive.Bounds as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/DepthThree.lean
@decl OAI.DepthThreeLowerBound.exists_polynomial_time_language_depth_three_lower_bound
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Beyond the square-root exponent for depth-three circuits
-- L4DEL: intuition: The formalized result gives one polynomial-time Boolean language whose exact depth-three OR–AND–OR circuit size eventually exceeds $2^{A\sqrt n}$ for every fixed $A>0$. The language and its polynomial-time algorithm are fixed before $A$ is chosen, while circuits may vary with the input length. Gates have unrestricted finite fan-in and sharing, with free input negations and constants. The result does not assert a fixed exponent $n^{1/2+\varepsilon}$ or a lower bound for unrestricted depth.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Computability.DepthThree.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

