@file ComparatorChallenges/SharpThreshold.lean
@decl OAI.Problem313.sharp_threshold_width
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Uniform influence and sharp thresholds for graph and hypergraph properties
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Friedgut–Kalai sharp-threshold conjecture concerns how quickly a nontrivial increasing graph property appears in the independent-edge random graph. For every $n\ge2$, every such property invariant under vertex relabeling, and $0<\varepsilon<1/2$, the formalization proves that the edge-probability interval between probabilities $\varepsilon$ and $1-\varepsilon$ has width at most $2^{19}\log(1/(2\varepsilon))/(\log n)^2$. The constant is uniform over the graph property, $n$, and $\varepsilon$.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Combinatorics.SharpThreshold.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/SidorenkoCounterexample.lean
@decl OAI.SidorenkoCounterexample.main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Counterexamples to Sidorenko’s conjecture and the forcing conjecture
-- L4DEL: intuition: Sidorenko's conjecture predicts that every bipartite graph $H$ has homomorphism density at least the host graph's edge density raised to $|E(H)|$. The formalization disproves this for the paper's fixed bipartite graph with $35$ vertices and $66$ edges: it constructs a nonempty finite simple host graph with $t(H,G)<t(K_2,G)^{66}$. The separate forcing-conjecture consequence in the paper is outside this statement.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Combinatorics.Sidorenko.RandomHost as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

