@file ComparatorChallenges/SurfaceConeCandidateSupport.lean
@decl OAI.SmallCM.candidate_ring_properties
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: A counterexample to the small Cohen–Macaulay module conjecture
-- L4DEL: intuition: The small Cohen–Macaulay module conjecture asks whether every complete local domain has a nonzero finitely generated maximal Cohen–Macaulay module. The linked formalization verifies only the ring-theoretic candidate: there exists a three-dimensional complete Noetherian integrally closed local domain over $\mathbb C$ whose residue field is $\mathbb C$. It does not prove that this ring lacks a nonzero finitely generated maximal Cohen–Macaulay module, or the paper's counterexample essentially of finite type.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.AlgebraicGeometry.SurfaceCones.CandidateRing as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/SurfaceImmersion.lean
@decl OAI.ClosedSurfaceR4.FiniteOrderSmoothing.smooth_isometric_immersion
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Smooth isometric immersions of surfaces into ℝ4
-- L4DEL: intuition: The formalization proves that every closed smooth Riemannian surface admits a smooth isometric immersion into $\mathbb R^4$. The differential preserves the Riemannian inner product on every tangent space. No orientability assumption is imposed, and the statement asks for an immersion rather than an embedding.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Geometry.SurfaceImmersion.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

@file ComparatorChallenges/SwitchChain.lean
@decl OAI.Problem315.switch_chain_main
-- L4DEL: title: Challenge statement: Rapid mixing of graph switches for every degree sequence
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Kannan–Tetali–Vempala conjecture asks for polynomial mixing of the switch chain for every graphical degree sequence. The formalization proves the simple undirected case: for $n\ge4$, the lazy chain that proposes switches on four vertices has total-variation mixing time at distance $1/4$ at most $2n^8$. It also proves switch connectivity, the stated exponential total-variation bound, and a spectral gap of at least $1/(24n^2\binom n4)$ when more than one graph realizes the degree sequence. The paper's exactly uniform sampling algorithm is outside these selected chain estimates.
-- L4DEL: step: This is an exact comparator target linked by the pinned repository's formalization note for this result.
-- L4DEL: step: The repository's comparator configuration selects OAI.Probability.SwitchChain.Main as the released solution module checked against this declaration.

